    43	## 1Loops, for the cold reader
    44	
    45	 Loops is a design-memory substrate. People and agents emit facts —
    46	 decisions, open threads, observations, frictions — into a vertex, which is
    47	 a named store plus a fold schema. Facts are immutable and append-only; a fold
    48	 maintains a compressed current view (latest decision per topic, current status per thread), so a
    49	 session reads the fold to orient and emits as it works. Correction is re-emission, never editing;
    50	 the history is the point.
    51	
    52	 Around that core sit the evidentiary mechanics: every fact is attributed to a signing
    53	 observer; a tick is a sealed checkpoint — a signed,
    54	 hash-chained record that commits to the exact window of facts it closed over. Stores can be
    55	 shared between people and machines, and reads can be addressed at a
    56	 witness position: “answer as of what this store had received at
    57	 point P,” not just “answer now.”
    58	
    59	 That last idea — that when a store received something is first-class,
    60	 inspectable history — is what this whole proposal is about getting structurally right.
    61	
    62	## 2Where this came from
    63	
    64	A performance fix pulled a thread, and the thread was load-bearing.
    65	
    66	 PR #8 made fold replay follow receipt order — the order this store
    67	 received facts — instead of event-timestamp order, collapsing an O(n²) ingest into a
    68	 linear one (a fold over a prefix-stable order can resume from a checkpoint; a fold over
    69	 timestamp order must replay whenever a backdated fact lands in the middle). That fix worked, but
    70	 it raised the real question: what is receipt order, authoritatively?
    71	
    72	 Today the answer is embarrassing when said out loud. The canonical artifact is a JSONL file
    73	 — intended to be tracked and shared through Git, though that tracking was never actually
    74	 realized — the sqlite database beside it is a derived index; and custody order is
    75	 implicit in line order, materialized as sqlite’s rowid. The
    76	 audit that closed this arc found the latent defect that implies: the tick chain’s
    77	 cryptographic commitments — window hashes, cursors — are computed over
    78	 rowid ranges, and a routine index rebuild regenerates that axis. Custody
    79	 commitments rest on an axis a maintenance operation reassigns. Worse, the canonical file is the
    80	 one git is invited to merge — and a textual merge of a custody log rewrites the custody.
    81	
    82	 Two intermediate designs were tried and dissolved on review (a two-mode
    83	 “receipt store vs. event store” scheme, then an arrival-record sidecar
    84	 bolted to it). Each round of cross-family review — two Claude models and a GPT model
    85	 deliberately kept independent, with a 22-agent repo audit between passes — removed a mode
    86	 or a special case, until one artifact remained.
    87	
    88	## 3The collapse
    89	
    90	The store is what this custodian received. Everything else is a question asked of it.
    91	
    92	 The canonical store becomes an append-only, lineage-local arrival log. It holds
    93	 complete canonical records plus a minted lineage genesis — the
    94	 custodian’s own identity marker. Its position is the witness position:
    95	 (arrival_lineage, ordinal). It is never merged, never rebuilt, never rewound.
    96	
    97	 Everything else is a projection of an arrival prefix: the sqlite index,
    98	 full-text search, fold state, graph views, checkpoints — and, crucially, the
    99	 JSONL file itself — today’s authoritative artifact, intended to be tracked
   100	 and shared through Git though that tracking was never actually realized — which becomes a deterministic, canonically-ordered
   101	 synchronization projection. Authority inverts: the file people diff, review, and share stops
   102	 being the store and becomes a derived representation of it.
   103	
   104	 [figure omitted]
   105	
   106	 The authority inversion. Today git merges write into the canonical artifact, and
   107	 custody order is whatever line order results. Under arrival, exactly one artifact is
   108	 canonical and git only ever touches a derived projection; the single write path into custody
   109	 is the custodian’s own admission step.
   110	
   111	### Three clocks, three authorities
   112	
   113	 The model keeps a strict ontology of time, and it survived every pass unchanged. Each temporal
   114	 datum has exactly one authority, and no datum ever substitutes for another:
   115	
   116	 | Clock | Field | Authority | What it means
   117	
   118	 | Claim | fact.ts | Claimant | “This is when I say the thing happened.” Observer-asserted event time. Data, never substrate.
   119	
   120	 | Emission | fact.id | Emitter | Identity and dedup (a ULID). Totalizes orderings deterministically; carries no time authority.
   121	
   122	 | Witness | (lineage, ordinal) | Custodian | “This is when I received it, relative to everything else I received.” Order, not wall-clock.
   123	
   124	 Wall-clock at the custody layer exists in exactly one place: a seal. A tick
   125	 binds its own signed timestamp to a witness position, so “this store had received X by
   126	 time T” is an attested claim with a chain behind it — not a bare field
   127	 anyone could have written. (Section 7 covers why the model deliberately adds no per-record
   128	 receipt timestamp.)
   129	
   130	## 4The five laws
   131	
   132	 The smallest attackable surface. Everything else in the record is derivable from these;
   133	 break one and you break the architecture.
   134	
   135	Law 1
   136	
   137	The store is the arrival log. Projections compute answers; arrival determines what those answers are about.
   138	
   139	FORBIDS — any local semantic or integrity claim resting on an artifact other than arrival. Reads may execute through sqlite, FTS, or a checkpoint — that is what they are for. If a projection disagrees with arrival, the projection is stale or invalid, never the other way around.
   140	
   141	Law 2
   142	
   143	Ordering over an arrival basis is witness order or a declared (thing, id) order. id totalizes and carries no domain-time authority.
   144	
   145	FORBIDS — the substrate learning what ts, fiction_time, or priority mean; and id being read as a clock. The second prohibition matters more after the generalization, because most declared keys are not time at all.
   146	
   147	Law 3
   148	
   149	Drivers propose candidates; only the custodian admits records and mints witness positions.
   150	
   151	FORBIDS — two things minting positions. A driver that can append to arrival is a second custodian, and the single-custodian invariant is the one property arrival exists to hold. The inbound interface is deliberately weak: it returns candidates, it does not write.
   152	
   153	Law 4
   154	
   155	Witness positions compare only within one arrival lineage.
   156	
   157	FORBIDS — cross-lineage comparison answering at all. It is not false, it is undefined, and it must fail structurally rather than return a confident wrong answer. Copying arrival bytes for backup or replication is legal and recommended; the typed error is opening a copied arrival log as another custodian while silently claiming continuation of the lineage. Restore is an explicit lineage act, never a silent one.
   158	
   159	Law 5
   160	
   161	Projection maintenance is derived from ordering semantics and reduction algebra. It is never an independent store mode.
   162	
   163	FORBIDS — a freely-declared maintenance class. A reduction wrongly marked order-independent produces silently wrong fold state — the only silent failure shape this architecture permits, so the claim is checked, not trusted (§5).
   164	
   165	 The laws were frozen with a standing reopening bar, so future review needs
   166	 evidence rather than aesthetics:
   167	
   168	Falsification criterion
   169	
   170	 Show a required Loops operation that cannot be expressed as custody in one lineage
   171	 plus declared interpretation/projection over an arrival basis, without introducing
   172	 false historical claims. Five review passes produced none.
   173	
   174	## 5Ordering, generalized
   175	
   176	“Event order” was only ever (ts, id) — and ts is just the first key anyone thought of.
   177	
   178	 The old design treated timestamp order as a special mode of the store. The generalization that
   179	 finished the collapse: fiction_time, effective_date,
   180	 priority, (chapter, scene) are all peers of ts. So
   181	 “event” leaves the vocabulary entirely and the substrate exposes one read
   182	 primitive:
   183	
   184	ordered(prefix = W, key = K)
   185	 = stable total ordering of arrival[0:W] by (K(record), record.id)
   186	
   187	 A projection declares order arrival or order by KEY. The substrate
   188	 never learns what any key means — domain vocabulary stays in declarations —
   189	 and id serves only as the deterministic tiebreak.
   190	
   191	 [figure omitted]
   192	
   193	 One prefix, two declared orders. Record 6 arrived late but belongs early in fiction
   194	 time. The arrival-order fold never re-opens folded state; the fiction-time projection pays a
   195	 bounded replay from the insertion point. Which cost a projection pays is derived,
   196	 never chosen.
   197	
   198	### Maintenance is derived, never declared
   199	
   200	 A projection has two declared inputs and one derived property:
   201	
   202	 | Ordering (declared) | Reduction (semantics) | Maintenance (derived)
   203	
   204	 | arrival | any, within the fold contract | prefix-stable — resume from head
   205	
   206	 | (K, id) | order-independent | insertion-independent — incorporate directly
   207	
   208	 | (K, id) | order-sensitive | insertion-sensitive — checkpoint + replay
   209	
   210	 Worked instances, and note the shape they make: FTS and the shared JSONL land in
   211	 different maintenance classes though both are non-fold projections; the fiction fold
   212	 and the shared JSONL land in the same class though one is a fold and one is a file.
   213	 Maintenance is a property of the (ordering, reduction) pair, never of the artifact.
   214	
   215	 | Projection | Order | Maintenance
   216	
   217	 | receipt-order fold | arrival | prefix-stable
   218	
   219	 | latest-by-event-time | (ts, id) | insertion-independent (max needs no replay)
   220	
   221	 | fiction chronology fold | (fiction_time, id) | insertion-sensitive
   222	
   223	 | full-text search | none meaningful | insertion-independent
   224	
   225	 | canonically ordered shared JSONL | (id) | insertion-sensitive
   226	
   227	 Why derivation is a law and not a style preference: a reduction wrongly asserted
   228	 order-independent yields silently wrong fold state — no error, no refusal.
   229	 Every other wrong answer in this architecture is engineered to fail structurally, so this one
   230	 claim gets a ratchet: where a user asserts order-independence, the test suite
   231	 runs the reduction over shuffled visitation orders of one record set and requires identical
   232	 results. A claim that can be falsified automatically must be.
   233	
   234	## 6The custody boundary
   235	
   236	Admission records observation, not semantic eligibility.
   237	
   238	 If arrival is permanent, irrecoverable evidence, the question “what may enter it?”
   239	 is the architecture’s most consequential boundary. The ruling separates two questions
   240	 that older designs fused:
   241	
   242	custody: did this custodian receive a legitimate assertion?
   243	interpretation: should this assertion participate in this answer?
   244	
   245	The admission table answers only the first, and it is complete:
   246	
   247	 | Verdict | Case | Why terminal
   248	
   249	 | reject | malformed bytes | re-obtaining changes nothing
   250	
   251	 | reject | invalid signature | re-obtaining changes nothing
   252	
   253	 | reject | id collision, different bytes | identity corruption; detectable via signatures
   254	
   255	 | reject | source not authorized for intake | source still holds the records if policy changes
   256	
   257	 | admit | everything else | — it arrived; that is a fact about this custodian
   258	
   259	 Admit does not mean believe. It means only: this custodian received this
   260	 legitimate assertion, at this position. Everything about semantic usefulness — trust,
   261	 curation, horizons, relevance — happens above admission, in declarations and
   262	 projections. A rejection is safely terminal if and only if the candidate remains
   263	 obtainable from its source — which the default transport guarantees (§8).
   264	
   265	 [figure omitted]
   266	
   267	 Drivers propose; the custodian admits. The inbound interface is deliberately weak: a
   268	 driver returns candidates and can never mint a position. Signature verification, intake
   269	 authorization, and position minting all sit on the arrival side of the seam.
   270	
   271	 Why some projections may participate in exchange and others may not: exchange bindings are
   272	 gated on being record-complete — a projection can carry canonical bytes
   273	 outward only if it preserves complete canonical records, which is why JSONL, Parquet, or a
   274	 full-record sqlite projection can serve as a transport surface while FTS or fold state cannot.
   275	 A lossy projection has already interpreted; letting it speak for the store would smuggle
   276	 interpretation back into custody.
   277	
   278	### The threat model this places correctly
   279	
   280	 An authorized peer can still assert pathological values — ts = 2099,

