   278	### The threat model this places correctly
   279	
   280	 An authorized peer can still assert pathological values — ts = 2099,
   281	 priority = −∞, effective_date = 1900. Under the
   282	 generalization this is not a timestamp problem: every declared key inherits it. A
   283	 pathological K captures latest-style (K, id) projections and forces
   284	 worst-case replay on insertion-sensitive ones. The ownership is deliberate:
   285	
   286	Arrival records who asserted it — nothing more.
   287	
   288	The projection’s declaration owns eligibility, horizon, and range policy for its own key.
   289	
   290	Maintenance machinery pays the replay cost its declared ordering implies — a priced cost, not a surprise.
   291	
   292	Signatures make abuse attributable: a hostile assertion is a visible, signed, permanent act by a chosen peer.
   293	
   294	 Rejecting such records at the custody boundary would make one projection’s taste into
   295	 everyone’s history filter — which is exactly the fusion of custody and
   296	 interpretation this architecture exists to remove.
   297	
   298	## 7The empirical evidence
   299	
   300	 The convergence was not taken on argument. The final pass attacked the frozen laws against
   301	 code, censused every temporal consumer in the tree, and ran the experiment three passes of
   302	 design had only reasoned about.
   303	
   304	### 7.1 — The merge experiment
   305	
   306	 Nobody had ever actually git-merged a JSONL store log. The final pass did, in a sandboxed lab:
   307	 seeded stores through the engine’s own APIs, cloned, diverged, and merged in every shape
   308	 git offers. Findings, with the working tree receipts preserved:
   309	
   310	 [figure omitted]
   311	
   312	 Receipt order stops being a property of the store and becomes a property of the
   313	 merger. This is the measured, stronger form of the argument for a monotonic transport
   314	 — it replaced the assumed “lines interleave unpredictably” story.
   315	
   316	The individual findings, each with verbatim receipts in the lab transcript:
   317	
   318	 Rebuild alone is safe — measured. A store that tailed forward, a store
   319	 that rebuilt, and a fresh clone with no index at all produce byte-identical row order and
   320	 identical window hashes from the same log. The invalidator is a rebuild over a log whose
   321	 line order changed; rebuild over an unchanged log is order-isomorphic.
   322	
   323	 The silent cursor failure is real. After pulling a union merge, a held
   324	 durable handle resolved without any error to a different witness prefix — two
   325	 foreign facts now inside a position that previously excluded them; seq:5 named a
   326	 different record entirely. Nothing raised. A plausible answer under silently changed
   327	 semantics.
   328	
   329	 Chain verification is loud only inside sealed windows. The same merge
   330	 produces three loud breaks when foreign lines land inside a sealed window — and a clean
   331	 ok: true when they land past the last seal, indistinguishable from honest
   332	 unsealed appends. The unsealed tail is exactly where an active store lives.
   333	
   334	 The split-brain verify. A byte-length-preserving interior edit merges cleanly
   335	 under git’s default driver. The same commit then yields two different folds and
   336	 two different verify_chain verdicts in two working trees — the tree that
   337	 kept its index certifies a log it no longer matches.
   338	
   339	 The duplicate-id brick. Union-merging the same fact id sitting at two
   340	 different tail positions commits a store that no clone can ever open again —
   341	 discovered only after the merge has been committed and distributed. (Simulated through the
   342	 transport seam; today’s wired paths refuse before reaching it.)
   343	
   344	Why this evidence closed the argument
   345	
   346	 The two worst outcomes — the brick and the split-brain — are states the
   347	 arrival architecture cannot represent. An id-sorted shared projection gives one fact
   348	 one position by construction, and Law 1 makes a projection never a verify basis. The new
   349	 model doesn’t handle these failures better; it makes them inexpressible.
   350	
   351	### 7.2 — The last open question, and how it closed
   352	
   353	 One custody-schema question survived four passes: should arrival hold a typed
   354	 “deferred” entry for legitimate-but-not-yet-usable candidates, or a per-record
   355	 receipt timestamp? The question sharpened into a falsifier — does
   356	 any real operation require historical wall-clock context at a witness position? —
   357	 and an exhaustive census of the tree answered it:
   358	
   359	Exactly one operation in the entire codebase maps wall-clock to a position
   360	 (the CLI’s --at <date> form), and snapping to the last sealed
   361	 checkpoint is its documented contract, not a degraded answer.
   362	
   363	Every other consumer of the seal-anchor machinery does identity and ordinal arithmetic
   364	 (“is this position sealed?”, “how many records past the seal?”) —
   365	 duties a timestamp cannot retire.
   366	
   367	Evidence-grade dating is already served better by seals — signed and
   368	 chain-committed, with seal density as the precision lever — than any unsigned field
   369	 could serve it.
   370	
   371	And the migration argument: arrival times for the legacy corpus are
   372	 unreconstructible, so the field would be born null for all history or fabricated — and
   373	 fabricating historical precision is expressly forbidden (§9).
   374	
   375	Resolution — ratified 2026-08-17
   376	
   377	 Add neither. Historical reconstruction is intentionally limited to
   378	 witness position + declaration generation + fact contents. The admission table has no
   379	 legitimate-but-inadmissible row for a deferral to hold, and retention obligations for
   380	 windowed transports belong to those drivers, never to arrival. Reopening requires a real
   381	 consumer, by the falsification criterion in §4.
   382	
   383	## 8Sharing over git
   384	
   385	The projection obeys the same law as what it projects.
   386	
   387	 Sharing stores through git is an intended, forcing workflow — per-repo curated vertices
   388	 plus combinatorial vertices over them. The ruling: the shared projection lives on a
   389	 monotonic ref (its own append-only line of history, published on push), not as
   390	 a branch-traveling tracked file. Four arguments, one decisive:
   391	
   392	A fact’s survival must not depend on a code-review outcome. An
   393	 observation made on an abandoned branch is more valuable, not less — it is the
   394	 record of a path not taken.
   395	
   396	Same law as the source. Arrival is append-only and never rewound; under a
   397	 monotonic ref, so is its published projection. Fast-forward is the superset check; a
   398	 rejected push is the conflict, resolved by re-project-and-push.
   399	
   400	Branch-travel loses its own motivating benefit. Facts do not belong to
   401	 branches: a checkout that drops admitted facts is a divergent projection, repaired by
   402	 re-projecting — so every branch’s copy converges to the local union anyway.
   403	 Branch-travel delivers a perpetually churning tracked file, not PR-scoped facts.
   404	
   405	Aggregates break under it. A combinatorial vertex over N repos would answer
   406	 differently depending on which branch each clone has checked out. Under a monotonic ref,
   407	 membership only grows.
   408	
   409	 The merge experiment’s block-ordering result (§7.1) is the empirical version of the
   410	 same point, and the ff-only shape it validates is precisely this transport. The semantic merge
   411	 driver designed in an earlier pass is deferred, not built — it is what a
   412	 branch-travel driver would need if someone ever configures one, and nothing is prepaid for it.
   413	
   414	## 9Shipping: 1.0, sidecar, new CLI
   415	
   416	One break, spent on the model actually believed.
   417	
   418	 The project’s standing rule is a one-break budget: the persisted store
   419	 contract may break once, loudly. That is semver-major reasoning, so the arrival substrate ships
   420	 as 1.0.0 — no intermediate store contract before it, and the interim
   421	 hazard documented on main (the merge behaviors of §7.1, currently unreachable) ends when it
   422	 lands.
   423	
   424	### Migration is a sidecar, not native
   425	
   426	 1.0 does not read 0.x stores. A separate, one-time migration sidecar converts
   427	 them. Supporting legacy formats natively-forever would keep two open code paths alive
   428	 indefinitely — the two-mode store re-entering through a compatibility door — so the
   429	 sidecar is the option that completes the dissolution. Three requirements keep it honest:
   430	
   431	Sidecar-ness relaxes the maintenance commitment, never the epistemics.
   432	 The migration is fully receipted; it reproduces exact historical receipt order where the
   433	 source contains it, and where it does not, it mints an explicit legacy-adoption genesis
   434	 marker — “historical receipt unavailable before this boundary” —
   435	 rather than inventing precision. Idempotent and resumable are release blockers.
   436	
   437	A bridge pinned at both ends. The last 0.x reader and the first 1.0 writer
   438	 ship as frozen, pinned dependencies — runnable years later (uvx) for a user
   439	 arriving from any 0.x. A frozen artifact, never a maintained surface.
   440	
   441	Provenance lives in the store, not the tool. The adoption receipt in the
   442	 new arrival log is what makes deleting the tool safe: the store carries its own migration
   443	 evidence after the sidecar is gone.
   444	
   445	 [figure omitted]
   446	
   447	 One-time, not support-forever. The only permanent residue in 1.0 is a legacy-shape
   448	 detector that refuses and names the sidecar — a location claim, never an
   449	 open-and-read path.
   450	
   451	### The CLI is rebuilt from scratch over the SDK
   452	
   453	 The current CLI grew up alongside the old authority model. Rather than retrofitting it, 1.0
   454	 ships a new CLI as a thin wrapper over the SDK, with explicit license to drop any current
   455	 concept that doesn’t fit the model. This is safe because the verification pyramid —
   456	 conformance vectors, architecture rules, property suites — lives at engine/SDK level and
   457	 survives; only CLI-level goldens rebuild, against a surface that is honest about the new model.
   458	
   459	### Dissolves
   460	
   461	store-file suffix as a mode switch (residence)
   462	
   463	the byte-prefix custody model in store verify (re-specified over arrival)
   464	
   465	merge/receive as sqlite operations — they become admission through drivers
   466	
   467	adopt reshapes into the lineage-genesis ceremony
   468	
   469	### Carried, or stronger
   470	
   471	witness addressing, re-keyed on (lineage, ordinal) — seq:N inverts from today’s most fragile address form to the canonical coordinate
   472	
   473	the read grammar, lenses, and zoom discipline
   474	
   475	the emit grammar and fold-key model
   476	
   477	seals, chain verification, signing — re-based on arrival positions
   478	
   479	 Implementation proceeds as four cuts: A authority (arrival becomes
   480	 canonical), B projections (sqlite and the shared JSONL re-derive from it),
   481	 C ordering (declared projection orders, (K, id) keys),
   482	 D surfaces (verification and seals re-specified over arrival).
   483	
   484	## 10What this costs
   485	
   486	The record keeps its bill visible; so does this document.
   487	
   488	 Arrival is simultaneously the most locally vulnerable artifact and the only
   489	 irrecoverable one. Lose the sqlite index — rebuild it. Lose the shared
   490	 projection — rematerialize it. Lose arrival, and “this custodian received these
   491	 records in this order” cannot be recreated from any surviving artifact; the facts are
   492	 re-adoptable only into a new lineage. The durability asymmetry inverts from
   493	 today. Mitigation falls out of append-only: incremental backup through a known durable head,
   494	 no snapshot coordination required.
   495	
   496	 The artifact humans read moves to the derived side. The git-visible file
   497	 — the thing reviewed and shared — is a projection, and readers will treat what
   498	 they can see as authoritative. The interface must disclose the inversion; the conceptual
   499	 model’s protected invariants are amended to require it (“derived artifacts never
   500	 masquerade as canonical ones — including the ones people look at most”).
   501	
   502	 Facts become residence-wide rather than branch-wide, permanently. The
   503	 branch-travel workflow some might expect (facts scoped to a PR) is structurally unavailable
   504	 — by argument §8.3, it always was.
   505	
   506	 Sync acquires an explicit admission boundary that did not exist before,
   507	 including signature verification on intake — new code, new operational surface.
   508	
   509	 Insertion-sensitive projections keep their replay bill. Declared
   510	 (K, id) orders pay checkpoint-and-replay when records sort into prior
   511	 material — a derived, visible cost rather than a mode choice, but a cost.
   512	
   513	## 11What remains open
   514	
   515	The burden of proof has moved to implementation. These are the named targets.
   516	
   517	Migration exactness is the first adversarial target. The architecture is
   518	 unusually strict about historical claims, and migration is where a correct design can betray
   519	 its philosophy by inventing data. The contract must establish, store by store, which permit
   520	 exact reconstruction and which take the explicit adoption boundary.
   521	
   522	Experiment residuals, not architectural blockers — the ruled transport is
   523	 monotonic fast-forward and the semantic merge driver is explicitly deferred, so these are
   524	 unanswered empirical questions from the old-log merge lab restated as implementation
   525	 obligations: signed-tick migration/rebasing onto arrival positions (all lab
   526	 ticks were unsigned), and aggregate reads across independently updated
   527	 arrival-backed members.
   528	
   529	The four cuts have not been attacked as a decomposition — nobody has
   530	 pressure-tested whether cut A is atomically shippable.
   531	
   532	The loops-go conformance oracle is out of tree and unaudited — the
   533	 most consistently flagged blind spot across every review pass.
