01M09GNGW7VTSKDPYMJ027HEPR	decision	design/arrival-slice0-record-grammar	RATIFIED (Kyle, 2026-08-17 session with Fable — the slice-0 ratify gate per plan:arrival-libs-slice-0 is now OPEN for implementation). The arrival log record grammar + suffix design fact, proposal doc: docs/dev/arrival-slice0-record-grammar-2026-08-17.md (authored by a Claude design agent over an agy-explorer ledger; all citations spot-verified).\n\nCORE: suffix .arrival (companions .arrival.lock / .arrival.tmp); JSON-lines framing inheriting the ensure_ascii/allow_nan=False encoder discipline; record fields in order v, lin, ord, prev, at, k, observer, origin, body, sig?, rh; coordinate (lin, ord) WRITTEN INTO the record — append validates, never assigns; rh = sha256(JCS(record-without-rh)); prev chains rh from slice 0 (deferring to slice D would leave a permanent chain hole); torn tail truncates ONLY under the append lock, corrupt interior always refuses; genesis is ordinary record at ord=0, k=genesis, sig REQUIRED, O_EXCL staging + atomic rename + directory fsync; append under fcntl.flock on .arrival.lock (mutual exclusion forced by ordinal assignment), reads lock-free; resume mark = (arrival_lineage, arrival_offset, arrival_ordinal), all three validated, counts custody DISSOLVES. NAMED DEPARTURE, ratified as such: body is a JSON object, not verbatim TEXT — the verbatim-payload invariant retires with the two-custody-holders condition that created it; JCS defines canonical bytes.\n\nKYLE'S THREE RULINGS: (1) sig covers CONTENT ONLY (k, at, observer, origin, body — field-for-field the existing commitment envelope) — coordinate-covering signatures would make identity store-local; portable single identity across many stores is the point. Coordinate integrity rides rh/prev. (2) Genesis body MINIMAL in slice 0 — protocol version + lineage claim; document set and migration pins are slice A's, where a genesis has real history behind it. (3) Suffix .arrival over .lineage after vocabulary workshop — lineage is a PROPERTY of the records; the logs are the arrival of observations. Axis name stays continuous across filesystem, SDK (ArrivalHead), and meta keys (arrival_*).\n\nVOCABULARY: seed denylist EMBEDDED per plan:arrival-vocabulary-ratchet (jsonl_canonical + jsonl_* meta family, receipt_order/receipt_mode/receipt-as-custody, event-as-ordering, reanchor-as-operation, merge-touches-custody, rewind, mergeability/union-discipline, two-CAS-species names — the last as a pointer slice C resolves against the ruled list). Glossary ratified: arrival log, record, lineage, ordinal, coordinate, genesis, head, append, resume mark, torn tail, projection, body. Named adjacency: witness.py late_arrivals is a different (witness-time) sense; slice A decides its rename.\n\nGATE (slice-0, empirical): six tests in proposal §3.5, headlined by 4 real OS processes x 250 appends asserting dense gapless ordinals + intact prev chain + recomputing rh. Real processes, not threads — the gate is not passed otherwise.
01M09JEHBY88QNS54TEQWEEDZ7	observation	test/arrival-slice0-gate-deviations	Slice-0 gate run COMPLETE, merged to feat/arrival-libs @ 2ad21d92 (slice branch slice/arrival-log, 4 commits, gated independently: suites re-run, one power proof re-broken by hand at the gate, production diff empty, fence held). TWO GATE DEVIATIONS ACCEPTED by Kyle 2026-08-17, both empirical findings not preference: (1) SIGKILL CANNOT TEAR AN APPEND on macOS/APFS — a whole-record single write() to a regular file is signal-uninterruptible (30 kill rounds, 0 torn tails; 300MB os.write returns full). Gate-2 torn tail is produced by RLIMIT_FSIZE instead — a REAL kernel short write. The SIGKILL result is PINNED as an asserting test (test_a_real_sigkill_cannot_tear_an_append) so Linux CI corrects us if it differs. DESIGN INSIGHT: the torn tail is a DURABILITY condition (power cut, short write), not a crash condition — strengthens design doc §2.6/§3.3. (2) Gate-6 "reader never sees a partial record" is STRUCTURAL (same single-write reason), not stressable live; claim split into live-concurrency half + deterministic torn-tail-left-alone sibling, both power-proven. Implementer's own proof-of-work bar caught two silent-pass breaks in the original live test — the bar working as designed. NOT YET VERIFIED: Linux/ubuntu CI behavior (lands with the eventual PR); real power-cut durability (untestable in harness, honestly named). Pyright nits (NoReturn annotation on _bad, two unused names) deferred to the flash-high review round.
01M09KPYEGSKXD824TAQ6XGXEQ	observation	test/arrival-slice0-review-ledger	SLICE-0 DISPOSITION LEDGER (running). Branch feat/arrival-libs. Round 1 review: gemini-3.7-flash-high, 4 findings, 4 dispositions out, ZERO dropped. All FIXED (none required a Kyle ruling; none re-litigated ratified ground). Remediation merged @ 5405f5b8.\n\nF1 FIXED — walk() enforced only kind at ordinal 0 while genesis() also demanded signature + self-naming body; a forged unsigned genesis walked clean. F2 FIXED — genesis-kind record accepted at interior ordinals on both append and read paths. DISSOLUTION: F1+F2 collapsed into ONE _placement_fault(record, ordinal) validator (arrival.py:344-385) — "the genesis kind belongs at ordinal 0 and nowhere else" — called from genesis/walk/_walk_tail/_check_follows, differing only in error class. Four parallel rule-sets with gaps between them became one rule stated once; read()/head() inherit it via walk(), test-asserted.\n\nF4 FIXED (sharpest) — resume_offset validated mark.arrival_lineage and the anchor's ord but never the ANCHOR RECORD's own lin, and _walk_tail derived its lineage expectation FROM the anchor. Exploit reproduced pre-fix: ordinals 2..4 re-chained onto a foreign lineage were accepted on the resume path with the attacker's lineage fully substituted, silently. Fix: fourth resume check + _walk_tail takes lineage as a parameter sourced from genesis. Claim scoped in docstring: the four checks do NOT establish the anchor chains back to genesis — that costs the O(n) walk the mark exists to avoid; ACCEPTED GAP, candidate for slice B.\n\nF3 FIXED — Rule 18 evadable (CamelCase/UPPERCASE/capitalized/dict-key-constant, probe-proven) + union_discipline missing. Now token-normalized (camel split, case fold, separator squash) with keys and subscript indices judged as names; 12 evasions + 6 negative controls as cases. TWO NARROWINGS, both the scope-the-claim rule: (a) ARBITER RULING — multi-word bans are IDENTIFIER bans not prose bans; the surfaced hit sdk/read.py:244 is accurate shipped behaviour already Rule-17-allowlisted, and condemning it would make Rule 18 a standing suppressed failure. §4.2's own wording bans identifiers/meta keys/field names/function names/glossary terms. Kyle may overturn. (b) sense-qualified bans (event/merge/receipt as ordering/custody concepts) deliberately NOT mechanized — a token scanner cannot read sense, and banning bare would fire on sdk's EmitReceipt; gap named in _NOT_MECHANIZED and asserted by a test so it cannot drift.\n\nPROCESS FINDING (method, worth keeping): `uv run --package engine` rebuilds per invocation, so rapid successive edits can run a test against a STALE package copy — and the failure mode makes a power-proof break look GREEN (uncaught), never red. Implementer added a liveness assertion (inspect.getsource confirms the interpreter imported the broken source) to its proof harness; the gate now uses the same check. Directional safety: every "caught" result stays sound; only "uncaught" results were ever suspect.\n\nGATE (arbiter, independent): scope clean, 78 arrival + 87 arch + 1614 engine green, one power proof re-broken by hand WITH liveness verified (F4 anchor-lineage check removed -> test_gate_an_anchor_carrying_a_foreign_lineage_is_rejected red; restored -> green), production diff empty, review worktrees swept clean both rounds. Round 2 (attack-the-fixes, with unverified-fixes enumeration) DISPATCHED. Convergence rule per Kyle: iterate until a round returns zero new findings with every prior finding dispositioned.
01M09MMZ7BFGM2BF5ZTWAR3E0X	observation	test/arrival-slice0-review-ledger	SLICE-0 DISPOSITION LEDGER — ROUND 2 CLOSED (supersedes the round-1 entry; branch feat/arrival-libs @ f07bfb90). Running totals: 7 findings in, 7 dispositions out, ZERO dropped. Rounds 1-2 both found real defects; not yet converged. Round 3 dispatched.\n\nROUND 2 (gemini-3.7-flash-high, pointed at BREAKING round-1's fixes): verdict NOT_CONVERGED, 3 findings, all confirmed, all FIXED @ f07bfb90.\nF1-R2 BLOCKING — resume path accepted an INVALID INTERIOR GENESIS as anchor and consumed past a record walk() refuses. F2-R2 BLOCKING — append validated the CANDIDATE's placement but never the HEAD's, so a log whose ordinal-0 record is unsigned/forged kept accepting appends (arbiter reproduced independently). F3-R2 MAJOR — Rule 18 missed __all__ exports, string forward-ref annotations, punctuation-separated dict keys.\n\nTHE ROOT CAUSE, and the reason this arc is worth receipting: a PROXY SHAPE. resume_offset validated an anchor that walk_from then INDEPENDENTLY RE-READ — the checks did not live at the point the record actually became an authority. That single shape produced round-1 F4 AND both round-2 blockers. Fixed as the general form, not the instances: _authority_fault(record, lineage) states what makes an existing on-disk record adoptable AS A PREMISE (decodes + rh recomputes + _placement_fault at its claimed ordinal + lineage matches), and there is now ONE adoption site each — _anchor_for(mark) for the resume anchor, _tail_record for the append head (reads genesis first to establish the true lineage). EVIDENCE THE GENERAL FORM WAS THE RIGHT UNIT OF REPAIR: writing it caught a THIRD instance no review reported — a tail re-chained onto a foreign lineage accepted appends, because the candidate copies its lineage FROM the head and the head was never compared to genesis. Point-fixing the two reported instances would have left that for round 3.\n\nDISTINCTION NOW LOAD-BEARING IN THE DESIGN: reading a record forward and adopting one as a premise are DIFFERENT ACTS. A rule enforced on the way past a record says nothing about a record you never walked to. The lazy/eager split is why: walk() is a generator that validates nothing until consumed (now docstring'd + test-pinned), and the O(1) eager paths that adopt authorities were skipping what the consumed read path performs.\n\nTHREE ARBITER RULINGS overnight, all overturnable by Kyle: (1) multi-word denylist terms are IDENTIFIER bans not prose bans; (2) sense-qualified bans (event/merge/receipt) deliberately NOT mechanized, named in _NOT_MECHANIZED; (3) the O(n) residual ACCEPTED at its stated width — an adopted authority is not proven to chain back to genesis nor that its claimed ordinal is its physical position; both need the whole-prefix walk these O(1) paths exist to avoid, matching ratified §3.2 ("the mark is a cheap boundary check; walk is the integrity statement"). Round 3 is asked to test whether the residual is WIDER than stated, not to re-report it.\n\nQUEUED FOR SIMPLIFY (not defects): _tail_record reads genesis on EVERY append — the genesis lineage is immutable for the file's life, so it is memoizable; pyright nits (_bad lacks NoReturn so callers show possibly-unbound; unused names).\n\nGATE (arbiter, independent, round 2): both exploits re-run by hand and confirmed closed — F2-R2 refuses with GenesisRefused leaving bytes BYTE-IDENTICAL, F1-R2 discards the mark (resume_offset 0) and walk_from raises ArrivalCorrupt at ordinal 1; 84 arrival + 98 arch + 1620 engine green; one power proof re-broken by hand (placement half of _authority_fault) with liveness asserted, two tests caught it; production diff empty; review worktree swept clean all three dispatches.
01M09NZKTC2EE13W5FGQ6PDNYB	observation	test/arrival-slice0-review-ledger	SLICE-0 CONVERGED 2026-08-18. Branch feat/arrival-libs @ 3b303d5d (five --no-ff merges: impl, remediation r1, remediation r2, simplify). FINAL: 7 findings in, 7 dispositions out, ZERO dropped. Round 3 (attack round-2's fixes) CONVERGED zero-findings; simplify pass ran; post-simplify re-review CONVERGED zero-findings. Suites 85 arrival + 98 arch + 1621 engine, pyright 0/0 on all four fenced files, ruff clean.\n\nROUND CURVE: r1 four findings (all minor-to-real), r2 three findings (TWO BLOCKING) found by attacking r1's fixes, r3 zero, post-simplify r4 zero. The descending curve is the convergence evidence; stopping after r1 would have shipped two blocking defects, which is the empirical argument for Kyle's iterate-until-dry ruling.\n\nTHE ARC'S ONE REAL LESSON, now written into the module: READING A RECORD FORWARD AND ADOPTING ONE AS A PREMISE ARE DIFFERENT ACTS. A rule enforced on the way past a record says nothing about a record you never walked to. One PROXY SHAPE (validate in resume_offset, re-read in walk_from) generated THREE separate vulnerabilities across two rounds; fixing the general form (_authority_fault + one adoption site each for anchor and head) caught a FOURTH instance no reviewer reported. Second-order: walk() is a generator, so the lazy read path validates nothing until consumed while the eager O(1) paths adopt authorities — that asymmetry is the structural reason the class existed. Now docstring'd and test-pinned.\n\nSIMPLIFY PASS (measured, not inferred): walk()/_walk_tail() carried line-for-line identical verification loops — both remediation rounds had to add the same check twice and F2-R2 nearly was patch-one-miss-the-other; unified as _verify_from (r4 proved behaviour-identical over 7 distinguishing cases). Genesis read on the append hot path memoized ON THE GENESIS LINE'S RAW BYTES: 149.4 -> 120.4 us/append (19%) with every check intact; a hit is not a skipped validation because the key IS the validated input. Path/inode/mtime keys explicitly REJECTED as F2-R2-reintroduced-as-optimization — that trap has its own test (test_the_lineage_memo_cannot_launder_a_tampered_genesis), which a weaker-keyed cache passes every other test and fails only this one. NEAR-MISS WORTH KEEPING: pyright called an isinstance branch in _validate unreachable; it is LIVE and the annotation was wrong — deleting the "dead" branch would have removed a real refusal with every test still green except its own. Fix the annotation, not the symptom.\n\nARBITER RULINGS (three, overnight, all await Kyle's confirmation and are overturnable): (1) multi-word denylist terms are IDENTIFIER bans not prose bans (sdk/read.py:244 "receipt order" prose is accurate shipped behaviour, Rule-17-allowlisted; condemning it makes Rule 18 a standing suppressed failure); (2) sense-qualified bans (event/merge/receipt as ordering/custody concepts) deliberately NOT mechanized, named in _NOT_MECHANIZED with a drift test; (3) the O(n) residual ACCEPTED at its stated width — an adopted authority is not proven to chain back to genesis nor that its claimed ordinal is its physical position; both need the whole-prefix walk these O(1) paths exist to avoid, matching ratified §3.2. r3 was asked to test whether the residual is WIDER than stated and found it is not. Also arbiter-ruled: probe.py suffix-dispatch arm DEFERRED to slice A (slice 0 is the standalone primitive; arrival is not a residence mode until authority moves); Rule 18 NOT widened to DictComp/ExceptHandler/match nodes (no forcing case — noted in docstring instead).\n\nCI COVERAGE CONFIRMED (closes the recurring "macOS only" caveat as far as it can be closed pre-PR): both new suites are auto-discovered by existing ubuntu CI with NO workflow change — check-architecture runs ./dev check -> pytest tests/ (Rule 18), and the Test (engine) matrix runs pytest libs/engine/tests (both arrival suites, incl. the 4-process gate, the RLIMIT_FSIZE torn tail, and the pinned SIGKILL-cannot-tear assertion). The Linux answer therefore arrives automatically at PR time, and the pinned test is built so a differing Linux result CORRECTS us rather than passing silently.\n\nSTILL UNVERIFIED, honestly: Linux (until CI runs); real power-cut durability (untestable in harness); NFS/network-mount flock semantics (explicitly out of contract per §3.3).
01M09P12JKHH6J5QWBK6A8V10B	thread	arrival-migration-arc	WAVE 1 SLICE 0 COMPLETE AND CONVERGED (2026-08-18, overnight run). feat/arrival-libs @ 3b303d5d holds the arrival log primitive: grammar, genesis, append under lock, ordinal read, torn-tail handling, resume mark, Rule 18 vocabulary ratchet. Design fact decision:design/arrival-slice0-record-grammar @ 01M09GNGW7 (Kyle-ratified, three rulings: sig content-only, minimal genesis body, .arrival suffix). Full review ledger at observation:test/arrival-slice0-review-ledger @ 01M09NZKTC; pipeline retrospective at retrospective:antigravity-impl-pipeline-first-live-run @ 01M09P0DM5.\n\nNEXT ENTRY POINT: CUT A — AUTHORITY (plan:arrival-libs-slice-A). The slice-0 ratify gate is discharged, so cut A may now launch. Its scope is already ruled: residence.py funnel grows the arrival answer (suffix switch at residence.py:56-62 is the whole mode flip), 113 libs-side funnel references swept, own_lineage's two writer ceremonies (absorb_genesis sqlite_store.py:932-935, adopt_lineage :1392-1395) re-point to arrival genesis, apps/ diff-empty enforced per-slice. GATE: the authority test answered literally — delete every projection, everything still answers from arrival — plus the libs/sign + observer-key registry file-position trace.\n\nCARRIED INTO CUT A FROM SLICE 0 (do not lose these):\n- The probe.py suffix-dispatch arm for .arrival was DEFERRED here by arbiter ruling. Slice 0 is the standalone primitive; arrival becomes a residence mode in cut A. This is the first thing cut A wires.\n- witness.py's late_arrivals naming adjacency: slice A decides whether it renames (different, witness-time sense of "arrival" — flagged in the design doc §4.2, deliberately untouched).\n- The vocabulary ratchet grows per Kyle's amendment: Rule 18's _SCAN_TARGETS gains a module in the slice that moves custody INTO it, so cut A adds its modules and shrinks the legacy allowlist accordingly. _NOT_MECHANIZED (event/merge/receipt as sense-qualified bans) stays unmechanized absent a forcing case.\n- The load-bearing distinction slice 0 established, which cut A must preserve as it re-points readers: READING A RECORD FORWARD AND ADOPTING ONE AS A PREMISE ARE DIFFERENT ACTS. Every path that adopts an on-disk record as an authority validates it at the adoption site (_authority_fault); every new reader cut A introduces inherits that obligation.\n- Accepted residual, unchanged: an adopted authority is not proven to chain back to genesis nor that its claimed ordinal is its physical position (both need the O(n) walk the O(1) paths avoid). If cut A's authority test wants that closed, it is a deliberate trade and Kyle's ruling.\n\nAWAITING KYLE (morning review, none blocking cut A's start): confirm or overturn three arbiter rulings — identifier-vs-prose denylist scope, unmechanized sense-qualified bans, and the accepted O(n) residual.
01M0AXD5KZMPXRTRFA5KP73SCM	thread	arrival-migration-arc	SLICE-0 ARBITER RULINGS ALL CONFIRMED BY KYLE (2026-08-18 morning review, walked with Fable): (1) Rule 18 multi-word denylist terms are identifier bans, not prose bans — sdk/read.py:244 prose stays Rule-17-allowlisted; (2) sense-qualified bans (event/merge/receipt as ordering/custody senses) stay unmechanized in _NOT_MECHANIZED with the drift test, mechanize only on a forcing case with the instance as fixture; (3) the O(n) residual ACCEPTED AS STATED after full unpack — adoption proves decode+rh, placement, and genesis-lineage match, deliberately not chain-to-genesis nor ordinal=physical-position; append/resume are availability paths, walk() is the integrity statement; the open threat is delayed detection (appends landing on an already-damaged file), not silent forgery, since an unsigned suffix was never walk-proof against a write-access adversary anyway. Middle paths (verified checkpoint on the resume mark, amortized walk) noted for cut A's authority test if it wants earlier detection — deliberate addition, not a bug fix. Local re-verify this morning: 119 tests green on feat/arrival-libs @ 3b303d5d. The slice-0 awaiting-Kyle item is fully discharged; nothing stands before CUT A.
