   888	            raise GenesisRefused(f"{self.path} does not exist") from exc
   889	
   890	    def _genesis_from(self, fh) -> dict:
   891	        """The validated genesis record, memoized on the genesis LINE BYTES.
   892	
   893	        The append path needs the lineage on every write, and re-deriving it
   894	        costs a JSON decode, an ``rh`` recompute and the genesis checks —
   895	        about a tenth of an append, which is worth not paying twice for the
   896	        same bytes.
   897	
   898	        **A cache hit is not a skipped validation.** The key is the exact
   899	        line the validated answer was derived from, so a hit means "these
   900	        bytes already passed, and validating them again is a pure function

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba libs/engine/src/engine/arrival.py | sed -n '900,1380p'" in /Users/kaygee/Code/loops
 succeeded in 0ms:
   900	        bytes already passed, and validating them again is a pure function
   901	        returning the same result". That is the only cache shape allowed
   902	        here: the standing lesson of this module is that an adopted
   903	        authority gets validated at the adoption site, and a cache keyed on
   904	        anything weaker than the content — a path, an inode, an mtime —
   905	        would be a way to skip that. Reading the line to compare it costs
   906	        about 0.1 µs against the 15 µs it saves, so the strong key is also
   907	        the cheap one.
   908	
   909	        Staleness therefore cannot bite: the instance may outlive a
   910	        truncation, a re-mint at the same path, or a foreign edit, and every
   911	        one of those changes the bytes, misses, and revalidates. A
   912	        replacement whose genesis is byte-identical opens the same lineage,
   913	        so the cached answer is still the right one.
   914	        """
   915	        fh.seek(0)
