SLICE0 DOC
     1	# Slice-0 proposal — the arrival log: file suffix, record grammar, append/resume contract
     2	
     3	Status: RATIFIED (Kyle, 2026-08-17) — design fact
     4	`decision:design/arrival-slice0-record-grammar` @ 01M09GNGW7VTSKDPYMJ027HEPR.
     5	Open questions in §5 are all ruled: (1) `sig` content-only — coordinate-covering
     6	signatures would make identity store-local; (2) minimal genesis body — documents
     7	and pins are slice A's; (3) suffix `.arrival` — lineage is a property of the
     8	records; the logs are the arrival of observations.
     9	Scope: `plan:arrival-libs-slice-0`. Grounded against the worktree at `406cec31`.
    10	All `file:line` citations below were read directly in
    11	`scratchpad/agy/wt-explore`, not taken from the exploration ledgers.
    12	
    13	This document proposes a **wire format**. Slices A–D and the wave-2 SDK build
    14	against it, and it is intended to be the eventual public surface. It is
    15	written to be boring on purpose: every choice is the explicit one, and the
    16	clever alternatives are listed under "rejected" with the reason.
    17	
    18	---
    19	
    20	## 0. What is being designed
    21	
    22	The arrival log is an append-only single file that holds **canonical custody**
    23	for a vertex store. A record's position in that file — its **ordinal** — is
    24	its identity coordinate. The SQLite index and the legacy line-per-row file
    25	become derived projections of it.
    26	
    27	Slice 0 delivers: the file format, the minted genesis record, append, read by
    28	ordinal, torn-tail handling, resume-from-offset, and a stated concurrency
    29	contract exercised by two real processes.
    30	
    31	Slice 0 does **not** deliver: authority (slice A), projection re-derivation
    32	(B), declared projection orders and compare-and-set (C), or audit/seal
    33	re-basing (D). Where slice 0 must leave a hook for a later slice, it is named
    34	below as a hook, not built.
    35	
    36	---
    37	
    38	## 1. File suffix
    39	
    40	### Proposed: `.arrival`
    41	
    42	A store named `alcove` holds its canonical custody in `alcove.arrival`.
    43	
    44	Companion paths, both derived by pure path arithmetic from the arrival path,
    45	mirroring the existing `sqlite_sidecars` / `intent_path_for` naming style
    46	(`libs/engine/src/engine/residence.py:97-105`,
    47	`libs/engine/src/engine/ceremony.py:110-113` — suffix appended to the full
    48	name, not `with_suffix`):
    49	
    50	| path | role |
    51	| --- | --- |
    52	| `<name>.arrival` | the log itself |
    53	| `<name>.arrival.lock` | the append-exclusion lock file (§3.3) |
    54	| `<name>.arrival.tmp` | genesis staging only, atomically renamed into place |
    55	
    56	**Why `.arrival`.** It carries the arrival vocabulary literally and reads
    57	correctly to someone who has never seen this codebase: the file is where
    58	records arrive. It collides with nothing in the existing inventory —
    59	`.jsonl` and `(.db, .sqlite)` (`residence.py:56-57`), `-wal`/`-shm`
    60	(`residence.py:97-105`), `.vertex` (`libs/engine/src/engine/probe.py:66`),
    61	`.intent` (`ceremony.py:92`), `.tmp` (`ceremony.py:131-133`), and
    62	`.recovery-lock` (`libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_store.py:417-418`). It also
    63	survives `probe.py`'s suffix dispatch (`probe.py:191-197`) untouched, so
    64	adding it is a new arm rather than a re-interpretation of an existing one.
    65	
    66	Note the `.recovery-lock` precedent is a *different* lock guarding index
    67	quarantine, on the index path, not the log path. `.arrival.lock` does not
    68	collide with it and must not be confused with it: they guard different
    69	resources and are held by different code.
    70	
    71	### Rejected alternates
    72	
    73	- **`.alog` / `.arl`** — abbreviations. They save four characters and cost a
    74	  reader the ability to guess what the file is. Explicit over clever.
    75	- **`.log`** — generic to the point of dishonesty. Every tool on the machine
    76	  has an opinion about what a `.log` is, and none of them is "canonical
    77	  custody, deletion loses the store". A rotating-logs script would eat it.
    78	- **`.custody`** — accurate about the role but not about the *mechanism*, and
    79	  the whole point of the arrival model is that custody is established by
    80	  arrival order. It also invites "custody" to become the vocabulary, which
    81	  starts a second name for the same thing.
    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	## 2. Record grammar
    86	
    87	### 2.1 Framing: JSON-lines, newline-terminated
    88	
    89	One record per line, UTF-8, terminated by `\n`. Not length-prefixed.
    90	
    91	The reason newline framing is *safe* rather than merely conventional is
    92	inherited, not assumed: the existing encoder discipline
    93	(`libs/engine/src/engine/jsonl_codec.py:123-131`) sets `ensure_ascii=True`,
    94	which keeps every emitted line 7-bit and, as its docstring states, free of raw
    95	U+2028/U+2029, and `allow_nan=False`, which refuses the non-JSON float
    96	constants. Under that discipline no encoded record can contain a raw newline,
    97	so a `\n` in the file is unambiguously a record boundary. The arrival encoder
    98	adopts the same three flags for the same reason.
    99	
   100	Length-prefixed framing was considered and rejected. It would let a record
   101	carry arbitrary bytes, which nothing here needs; it would make the file
   102	unreadable by `head`, `tail`, `wc -l`, and `grep`, which is a real operational
   103	cost on a file that is now the *only* copy; and it moves torn-tail detection
   104	from "the last byte is not `\n`" to "trust a length field written by the same
   105	interrupted write you are trying to detect". Self-describing framing is worth
   106	more than the bytes it costs.
   107	
   108	### 2.2 The record object
   109	
   110	Keys are emitted in this order. Order is transport encoding, not
   111	canonicalization — the same posture the current codec documents
   112	(`jsonl_codec.py:123-131`); canonicalization for hashing is JCS and is
   113	independent of emitted key order.
   114	
   115	| # | key | type | required | meaning |
   116	| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   117	| 1 | `v` | int | always | grammar version. `1` for this proposal. |
   118	| 2 | `lin` | string | always | the lineage this record belongs to. |
   119	| 3 | `ord` | int ≥ 0 | always | the arrival ordinal. Dense, gapless, starts at 0. |
   120	| 4 | `prev` | string \| null | always | `rh` of the record at `ord-1`; `null` iff `ord == 0`. |
   121	| 5 | `at` | number | always | wall clock at append. Authored content, signed. **Never an ordering input.** |
   122	| 6 | `k` | string | always | body discriminator — the record's kind. |
   123	| 7 | `observer` | string | always | who authored this record. |
   124	| 8 | `origin` | string | always | where it came from. May be `""`. |
   125	| 9 | `body` | object | always | the record's content. |
   126	| 10 | `sig` | string | only when signed | signature over the content commitment (§2.8). |
   127	| 11 | `rh` | string | always | this record's integrity hash (§2.4). Always last. |
   128	
   129	`observer` and `origin` are **top-level record fields**, not fields inside
   130	`body`. Completeness (§2.9) requires that a record read in isolation answers
   131	who authored it, which makes authorship structural in the same way the
   132	coordinate is structural — and it keeps the signed envelope's field list
   133	identical in shape to the one that exists today, so the continuity argument
   134	in §2.8 is a real continuity and not a resemblance. Their names are kept
   135	verbatim from `_fact_commitment_hash` (`sqlite_store.py:254-273`) for exactly
   136	that reason.
   137	
   138	Absent-not-null: `sig` is emitted only when the record carries one. This is
   139	the existing ethos, stated in `jsonl_codec.py` at lines 19-23 and enforced by
   140	the hashers' "include the signature key only when the row carries one"
   141	rule (`libs/engine/src/engine/sqlite_store.py:207-220`). A record with no
   142	signature and a record with `"sig":null` must not both be spellable, or they
   143	hash differently while meaning the same thing.
   144	
   145	`at` is called out explicitly because the ruled model says a record's id is
   146	never semantic time. `at` exists for humans and for forensics. No reader may
   147	sort by it, no projection order may reference it, and slice C's declared
   148	orders must not be able to name it. It is present because "when did this
   149	land" is a question the log should answer without a projection, and absent
   150	from every ordering rule because answering it is not the same as ordering by
   151	it.
   152	
   153	`at` **is** inside the signed envelope (§2.8), mirroring `ts` in the existing
   154	commitment. "Advisory" here means *advisory for ordering*, not unsigned: an
   155	author who states when a record was written is making a claim, and an
   156	unsigned timestamp is a claim anyone downstream can rewrite. The two
   157	properties are independent and both hold — signed, and never an ordering
   158	input.
   159	
   160	### 2.3 Addressing: `(lineage, ordinal)`
   161	
   162	`lin` and `ord` are stored **in the record**, not inferred from the record's
   163	position in the file.
   164	
   165	This is forced by slice A's completeness bar: if every projection is deleted,
   166	the file alone must still answer everything. An ordinal derived by counting
   167	lines is a fact about the *reader's* traversal, not about the record, and the
   168	moment two readers disagree about what counts as a line there are two
   169	answers. Written down, the coordinate is a property of the record and is
   170	checkable against the traversal rather than produced by it.
   171	
   172	Append therefore validates rather than assigns: a record may only be appended
   173	if its `ord` equals the tail record's `ord + 1` and its `lin` equals the tail
   174	record's `lin`. A reader that walks the file MUST check that ordinals are
   175	dense and ascending from 0 and refuse a file where they are not; a gap or a
   176	repeat is corruption, not a state the writer can produce.
   177	
   178	### 2.4 Record integrity: `rh`
   179	
   180	`rh = sha256(JCS(record-without-rh)).hexdigest()`, where JCS is RFC 8785 via
   181	the same `rfc8785.dumps` the existing commitment hashers use
   182	(`sqlite_store.py:141-155`). `rh` cannot cover itself, so it is computed over
   183	the object with `rh` removed and is emitted last purely as a reading
   184	convenience.
   185	
   186	`rh` earns its place by making torn and corrupt distinguishable (§2.6) and by
   187	giving `prev` something to point at.
   188	
   189	**A named departure: `body` is a JSON object, not verbatim TEXT.** The
   190	current codec's load-bearing invariant is that payload rides as the verbatim
   191	stored TEXT string, never re-serialized, never parsed by the codec
   192	(`jsonl_codec.py:8-14`), so that a line round-tripped through it re-derives
   193	byte-identical hashes and existing signatures keep verifying. I am changing
   194	that deliberately, and it should be reviewed as a change rather than read as
   195	an oversight.
   196	
   197	The invariant exists to solve a problem that only exists when there are two
   198	custody holders. Today the payload's authoritative form is a sqlite TEXT
   199	column, and the log is a second copy; re-serializing on the way through would
   200	let the two diverge byte-wise while agreeing semantically, and every hash
   201	would follow the divergence. In the arrival model there is no second copy —
   202	the log *is* the store, and every projection is derived from it — so there is
   203	no other byte-form for the object to disagree with. The object's canonical
   204	bytes are defined by JCS (RFC 8785, `sqlite_store.py:141-155`), which is
   205	already the canonicalization every commitment in this codebase hashes
   206	through and is a total function on the JSON value: any conforming
   207	implementation, in any language, derives the same bytes from the same value.
   208	That is precisely what the Go conformance oracle needs and what motivated the
   209	JCS upgrade in the first place.
   210	
   211	Keeping `body` as an opaque string would have preserved the letter of the old
   212	invariant while making the format worse: every body would be JSON encoded
   213	inside a JSON string, doubling the escaping, making the file unreadable by
   214	eye, and leaving the canonical bytes defined by whichever encoder happened to
   215	produce the string — the exact under-specification JCS was adopted to end. So
   216	the invariant is not being broken so much as retired along with the
   217	two-copies condition that created it, and slice A's migration must carry
   218	existing verbatim payload TEXT across the boundary explicitly, which is where
   219	the old and new forms actually meet.
   220	
   221	### 2.5 `prev` — chaining now, not later
   222	
   223	Each record names its predecessor's `rh`. Genesis carries `prev: null`.
   224	
   225	I am proposing this in slice 0 rather than deferring it to slice D, and it is
   226	the one place I am spending bytes ahead of a stated requirement. The reason
   227	is that the cost is asymmetric. Adding `prev` now costs one field and no
   228	extra I/O — append already reads the tail record to validate the ordinal
   229	(§2.3), so the predecessor's `rh` is in hand. Adding it in slice D costs a
   230	permanent discontinuity in the file: every record before the change has no
   231	predecessor link, and the chain slice D wants to walk has a hole in it that
   232	no later work can close. Slice D re-bases seals onto `(arrival_lineage,
   233	ordinal)`; a chain over that axis is exactly what it will reach for.
   234	
   235	Slice 0 does not *use* `prev` for anything beyond writing it correctly and
   236	checking it on append. Verification over the chain is slice D's.
   237	
   238	### 2.6 Torn tail vs. corrupt interior
   239	
   240	These are different conditions with different remedies, and the format must
   241	let a reader tell them apart.
   242	
   243	**Torn tail** — the file's last line does not end in `\n`. This is the
   244	interrupted-append window and nothing else, because appends are mutually
   245	excluded (§3.3), so at most one record can ever be partially written and it
   246	is always the last one. The remedy is truncation to the last newline, exactly
   247	the existing `_truncate_torn_line` rule and for the identical stated reason:
   248	partial bytes left at the tail would be concatenated onto by the next append
   249	(`jsonl_store.py:769-791`). The reverse-scan that finds the cut point
   250	(`_last_newline_before`, `jsonl_store.py:755-767`) migrates as-is; it is
   251	chunked and does not give up as a function of line length, which matters more
   252	here than it did there.
   253	
   254	**Corrupt interior** — any complete line that fails to decode, fails its
   255	`rh`, breaks the ordinal succession, or breaks the `prev` link. This is never
   256	truncated and never skipped. The reader refuses and asks for a human. This is
   257	the posture `IntentCorrupt` already takes for the same class of evidence:
   258	"a corrupt intent is evidence of a torn write or foreign tampering, and the
   259	safe answer is a human look, not a silent delete"
   260	(`ceremony.py:100-107`). On a file that is canonical custody, silent recovery
   261	is data loss with extra steps.
   262	
   263	The rule stated once: **truncate only what was never complete, and only at
   264	the tail.** Everything else refuses.
   265	
   266	A torn tail is also the reason `rh` is not sufficient alone and the newline
   267	is not sufficient alone. The newline tells you the record finished being
   268	written; `rh` tells you it finished being written *correctly*.
   269	
   270	### 2.7 The genesis record
   271	
   272	Genesis is an ordinary record under §2.2 with these additional rules:
   273	
   274	- `ord == 0` and `prev == null`. Both are forced; neither is a convention.
   275	- `k == "genesis"`.
   276	- `lin` is the lineage id this record opens, and the record is self-naming:
   277	  the lineage id is minted at genesis and the genesis record's own coordinate
   278	  is `(lin, 0)`. This preserves the existing rule that the genesis row's own
   279	  id *is* the lineage id (`sqlite_store.py:794-954`, step 4 of the docstring
   280	  and the `lineage_id = gen_id()` / returned `{"lineage": lineage_id}` path).
   281	  The difference is that the coordinate now carries it structurally instead
   282	  of by convention on an id column.
   283	- **`sig` is required.** A genesis with no signature is refused. This is the
   284	  existing `UnsignableGenesis` rule and its reasoning — genesis is the
   285	  lineage's attestation root — carried forward verbatim
   286	  (`sqlite_store.py:794-954`).
   287	- The signature is over the record's **final** body, never a throwaway probe.
   288	  The existing ceremony is explicit that "the signed commitment is over the
   289	  ACTUAL final payload"; keeping that means assembling the whole body,
   290	  signing it, and only then appending.
   291	
   292	A file whose first record is not a valid genesis is not an arrival log, and
   293	opening it refuses. There is no inference of identity from content: the
   294	existing code learned this the hard way and says so — the singleton heuristic
   295	that inferred a store's own lineage from its rows "was a hijack vector"
   296	(`sqlite_store.py:794-954`, step 1). In the arrival model that hazard
   297	dissolves rather than being defended against, because position 0 is
   298	structural and cannot be forged into existence by appending.
   299	
   300	Genesis is created under `O_EXCL` and published by atomic rename (§3.4).
   301	
   302	### 2.8 Signing: content commitment, unchanged
   303	
   304	The existing fact signature covers a **content-only** envelope — kind, ts,
   305	observer, origin, payload — and deliberately excludes id and rowid, which the
   306	docstring names as "custody context, not authored content", because that
   307	exclusion is what makes the signature transport-stable
   308	(`sqlite_store.py:254-273`).
   309	
   310	The arrival grammar keeps that split intact.
   311	
   312	**The signed envelope, stated as a field list.** `sig` is the signature over
   313	`sha256(JCS(envelope))` where the envelope is exactly:
   314	
   315	```
   316	{ "k": <k>, "at": <at>, "observer": <observer>,
   317	  "origin": <origin>, "body": <body> }
   318	```
   319	
   320	Five fields, no more. This is field-for-field the existing content commitment

