   327	rowid: `lin`, `ord`, `prev` are custody context, and `rh`/`sig` cannot be
   328	inside what they cover. A signature therefore survives a record being carried
   329	into another store, which is the property the existing design bought and
   330	should not be spent.
   331	
   332	- **`sig` signs the content commitment** above, and *not* the coordinate.
   333	- **`rh` covers the whole record including the coordinate**, so the
   334	  coordinate is tamper-evident even though it is not signed.
   335	- **`prev` chains the `rh`s**, so slice D binds seals to the arrival axis by
   336	  sealing over `(lin, ord, rh)` without needing the signature to reach into
   337	  custody.
   338	
   339	Whether the signature itself must additionally cover the coordinate is
   340	Kyle's to rule — see open question 1.
   341	
   342	### 2.9 What makes a record "complete"
   343	
   344	Slice A's bar: delete every projection and the file still answers everything.
   345	A record satisfies it when, read in isolation, it yields:
   346	
   347	1. **Where it sits** — `lin`, `ord`, and its link to what came before
   348	   (`prev`).
   349	2. **What it says** — `k` and `body`, with `body` self-contained. No
   350	   reference into a projection, no id that only resolves against SQLite, no
   351	   "see the index for the rest".
   352	3. **Who says it** — the authorship fields inside the commitment envelope,
   353	   plus `sig` where signed, verifiable against the observer registry without
   354	   consulting any projection.
   355	4. **That it is intact** — `rh`.
   356	
   357	Slice 0 enforces 1 and 4 structurally and validates 2 and 3 as well-formed.
   358	Making the *body* types complete for every kind the store holds is slice A's
   359	work; slice 0's obligation is that the frame can carry a complete record and
   360	refuses one that is structurally incomplete.
   361	
   362	---
   363	
   364	## 3. Append and resume contract
   365	
   366	### 3.1 Append durability
   367	
   368	Append is: acquire the lock (§3.3), read the tail record, validate the
   369	candidate's `ord`/`lin`/`prev` against it, encode, write, `flush`, `fsync`,
   370	release.
   371	
   372	`fsync` and not merely `flush`, for the reason the existing `_append_line`
   373	already gives: "the line must outlive a power cut, not merely a process
   374	crash" (`jsonl_store.py:515-527`). That reasoning was strong when the line
   375	was one of two copies. It is not optional now that it is the only one.
   376	
   377	**One strengthening over existing practice: fsync the containing directory
   378	when the arrival file is first created.** The existing code does not do this
   379	(`jsonl_store.py:515-527` fsyncs the file only), and on the old design that
   380	was survivable, because a lost-but-recreatable file could be re-exported from
   381	SQLite. Here it cannot: an fsync'd file whose *directory entry* never reached
   382	disk is a store that vanishes on power loss. The directory fsync costs one
   383	syscall, once, at genesis. Per-append directory fsync is not needed — the
   384	entry already exists and only the file's size and content change.
   385	
   386	Append is all-or-nothing at the record level. A record is either fully
   387	written and newline-terminated, or it is a torn tail that the next open
   388	truncates. There is no partial-record state a reader must interpret.
   389	
   390	### 3.2 Resume from offset
   391	
   392	A reader that consumed a prefix persists a **resume mark**, three values
   393	together:
   394	
   395	| field | meaning |
   396	| --- | --- |
   397	| `arrival_lineage` | which lineage this mark belongs to |
   398	| `arrival_offset` | byte offset just past the last consumed record's `\n` |
   399	| `arrival_ordinal` | the `ord` of the last consumed record |
   400	
   401	All three are validated on resume, and the mark is only trusted when all
   402	three agree:
   403	
   404	1. `arrival_lineage` matches the file's genesis `lin`. A mark from a
   405	   different lineage is not a stale mark, it is a mark about a different
   406	   file, and pointing it at this one is meaningless.
   407	2. `arrival_offset` lands strictly on a record boundary — the byte before it
   408	   is `\n` — and is within `0..size`. This is the existing `_prefix_intact`
   409	   boundary rule (`jsonl_store.py:793-830`) and the existing range check
   410	   (`jsonl_store.py:676-681`, which refuses a negative offset for the stated
   411	   reason that it "is not a seek position to try — it is metadata that cannot
   412	   be true").
   413	3. The record ending at `arrival_offset` has `ord == arrival_ordinal`.
   414	
   415	This third check is the one the current design cannot make and is the whole
   416	reason for storing the ordinal in the mark. Today the marker is a byte offset
   417	and a pair of counts, and validating it means decoding a line and comparing
   418	field values against the index (`_prefix_intact`, and the
   419	`_check_offset`/`_check_counts`/`_check_last_line` trio in
   420	`libs/engine/src/engine/canonical_audit.py:430-543`). With ordinals written
   421	into the records, the mark is checkable against **the log alone** — no
   422	projection required — which is the property that lets slice B rebuild
   423	projections from nothing.
   424	
   425	Note also what this drops. The current offset custody keeps *two* row counts
   426	alongside the offset (`canonical_audit.py:72-74`) because rows can enter the
   427	index without passing through the log, and the counts are how that is caught
   428	(`_refuse_out_of_band`, `jsonl_store.py:717-739`). In the arrival model that
   429	detector is not needed on the resume path: the ordinal is dense, so "how many
   430	records precede this one" is `ord`, by construction. Counts as a separate
   431	custody field dissolve. Whether an out-of-band *projection* write still needs
   432	its own detector is slice B's question, not slice 0's.
   433	
   434	A mark that fails any of the three checks is discarded and the reader starts
   435	from ordinal 0. Discarding a resume mark is cheap and always safe — it costs
   436	a re-read, never data — which is why it may be discarded silently where a
   437	corrupt *record* may not.
   438	
   439	### 3.3 Concurrency contract
   440	
   441	**Stated contract: appends to one arrival file are mutually exclusive across
   442	processes, enforced by an advisory exclusive lock on `<name>.arrival.lock`
   443	held for the whole read-tail-validate-write-fsync sequence. Reads take no
   444	lock and are safe at any time against a concurrent append.**
   445	
   446	Why exclusion rather than lock-free `O_APPEND`. `O_APPEND` gives atomic
   447	positioning, but it does not give what this format needs: the ordinal is
   448	assigned by reading the tail and adding one, so correctness requires the read
   449	and the write to be indivisible. Two processes that both read tail `ord=7`
   450	would both write `ord=8`, and the file would hold a duplicate coordinate — a
   451	corrupt-interior condition (§2.6) that refuses forever. Mutual exclusion is
   452	forced by the addressing scheme, not chosen for convenience.
   453	
   454	Why `fcntl.flock` specifically. It is the repo's proven mechanism for exactly
   455	this shape — an advisory `LOCK_EX` on a dedicated lock file guarding a
   456	critical section on a different file (`jsonl_store.py:417-420`). It is
   457	released automatically when the holding process dies, which matters: a
   458	crashed writer must not wedge the store. A lock file created with `O_EXCL`
   459	and deleted on release would have the opposite property — a crash leaves a
   460	stale lock and a human has to clean it up — which is right for a one-shot
   461	ceremony gate and wrong for an operation that runs constantly.
   462	
   463	Where `O_EXCL` *is* used: genesis creation (§3.4). "Exactly one process may
   464	ever mint this lineage" is a one-shot ceremony, and there `O_EXCL`'s
   465	crash-leaves-evidence behavior is the feature, matching the existing durable
   466	intent gate (`ceremony.py:451-456`).
   467	
   468	Readers taking no lock is safe because of the framing: a reader sees either a
   469	complete newline-terminated record or a tail it does not yet consider
   470	complete. A record being appended concurrently is simply not visible to the
   471	reader until its newline lands. A reader must therefore treat "no trailing
   472	newline" as *possibly in flight*, not automatically as torn — only the
   473	truncating writer, which holds the lock and knows no append is in progress,
   474	may conclude that a tail is torn and cut it. This is an important asymmetry
   475	and slice 0 must implement it: **truncation happens under the lock, never on
   476	the read path.**
   477	
   478	Known limitation, stated rather than hidden: `flock` is advisory and its
   479	semantics over NFS and some network filesystems are unreliable. The arrival
   480	log's concurrency contract holds on a local POSIX filesystem. A store on a
   481	network mount is out of contract, and slice 0 should say so in the module
   482	docstring rather than let someone discover it.
   483	
   484	### 3.4 Genesis creation
   485	
   486	Genesis is the one operation that cannot use the append lock, because the
   487	file it would lock the way to does not exist yet.
   488	
   489	1. Create `<name>.arrival.tmp` with `O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_WRONLY`, mirroring
   490	   `ceremony.py:451-456`.
   491	2. Write the genesis record, `flush`, `fsync`.
   492	3. `Path.replace` onto `<name>.arrival` — atomic publication, the existing
   493	   `ceremony.py:131-139` pattern.
   494	4. `fsync` the containing directory (§3.1).
   495	
   496	If `<name>.arrival` already exists, genesis refuses. Two racing processes:
   497	the first wins the `O_EXCL` on the tmp path or the `replace`; the second
   498	finds the file present and refuses. There is no window in which a partially
   499	written arrival file is visible under its real name.
   500	
   501	### 3.5 The slice-0 empirical gate
   502	
   503	The concurrency contract has never been empirically exercised in 0.x. The
   504	gate must use **real operating-system processes**, not threads — threads
   505	share a file descriptor table and a GIL and would pass a test that a fork
   506	would fail.
   507	
   508	1. **Concurrent append, dense ordinals.** N=4 `subprocess` writers × M=250
   509	   appends each against one arrival file. Assert afterwards: the file holds
   510	   exactly N×M+1 records; every line decodes; `ord` runs 0..N×M with no gap
   511	   and no repeat; every `prev` matches its predecessor's `rh`; every `rh`
   512	   recomputes. This is the test the whole design exists to pass.
   513	2. **Torn tail from a real kill.** A writer `SIGKILL`ed mid-append (forced by
   514	   an oversized body and a kill timed inside the write). Assert the file's
   515	   last line lacks `\n`, that the next open under the lock truncates exactly
   516	   those bytes and no more, and that the surviving prefix is intact and
   517	   append-able.
   518	3. **Corrupt interior refuses.** Flip a byte in an interior record's `body`.
   519	   Assert the reader refuses with a corruption error naming the ordinal, and
   520	   that it does **not** truncate. Repeat for a broken `prev` and for a
   521	   duplicated `ord`.
   522	4. **Genesis race.** Two `subprocess` genesis attempts against the same path.
   523	   Assert exactly one succeeds, the other refuses, and no `.tmp` survives.
   524	5. **Resume mark validation.** For each of: wrong lineage, offset mid-record,
   525	   offset past EOF, negative offset, ordinal disagreeing with the record at
   526	   the offset — assert the mark is rejected and the reader restarts from 0.
   527	6. **Reader during append.** A reader looping over the file while writers
   528	   append. Assert it never observes a partial record and never truncates.
   529	
   530	Test 1 is the gate. If it does not run as separate processes, slice 0 is not
   531	gated.
   532	
   533	---
   534	
   535	## 4. Vocabulary
   536	
   537	### 4.1 Proposed glossary
   538	
   539	Small on purpose. Every term below is either already the ruled arrival
   540	vocabulary or is introduced here with a reason.
   541	
   542	| term | meaning |
   543	| --- | --- |
   544	| **arrival log** | the append-only file that holds canonical custody. |
   545	| **record** | one line. The unit of atomicity, addressing, and integrity. |
   546	| **lineage** | the identity a genesis opens; the first half of a coordinate. |
   547	| **ordinal** | a record's dense position from 0; the second half. Never time. |
   548	| **coordinate** | `(lineage, ordinal)`. A record's identity. |
   549	| **genesis** | the record at ordinal 0; opens the lineage; must be signed. |
   550	| **head** | the highest ordinal currently in the log. |
   551	| **append** | the only mutation. Validate against head, write, fsync. |
   552	| **resume mark** | `(arrival_lineage, arrival_offset, arrival_ordinal)`. |
   553	| **torn tail** | an unterminated final record. The only truncatable state. |
   554	| **projection** | anything derived from the arrival log. Never authoritative. |
   555	| **body** | a record's content object, keyed by `k`. |
   556	
   557	Identifiers proposed for the format: `v`, `lin`, `ord`, `prev`, `at`, `k`,
   558	`body`, `sig`, `rh`. Meta keys proposed: `arrival_lineage`, `arrival_offset`,
   559	`arrival_ordinal`.
   560	
   561	### 4.2 Seed denylist — embedded in the design fact
   562	
   563	None of the following may appear in arrival identifiers, meta keys, field
   564	names, function names, or glossary terms. Ratified with this fact:
   565	
   566	- `jsonl_canonical` — and the `jsonl_*` meta-key family generally
   567	  (`jsonl_offset`, `jsonl_fact_count`, `jsonl_tick_count`).
   568	- `receipt_order`, `receipt_mode`, and `receipt` as an ordering or custody
   569	  concept.
   570	- **event** as an ordering concept.
   571	- **reanchor** as an operation.
   572	- **merge** as something that touches custody.
   573	- **rewind**.
   574	- **mergeability**, **union discipline**.
   575	- the two CAS species names from the prior design — as ruled in the
   576	  `plan:arrival-*` facts, not restated here (I was not given the literal
   577	  names; this entry is a pointer, not an ellipsis, and slice C must resolve
   578	  it against the ruled list before naming its two scopes).
   579	
   580	I have checked this document mechanically against that list. Every one of
   581	those terms occurs in exactly one place in this document — the list above.
   582	No proposed identifier, meta key, field name, or glossary term draws on any
   583	of them, and none appears in the design prose.
   584	
   585	One adjacency worth naming rather than discovering later: the word "arrival"
   586	already appears in existing prose and in one existing identifier —
   587	`late_arrivals`, a key in the interval diff report
   588	(`libs/engine/src/engine/witness.py`, exercised at
   589	`libs/engine/tests/test_diff_interval_report.py:55,76`). That is a *witness*
   590	concept about facts received late, not a custody coordinate, and it is not a
   591	suffix or a store symbol, so `.arrival` and the `arrival_*` meta keys do not
   592	collide with it. But the two senses of the word will sit in one codebase, and
   593	slice A should decide whether `late_arrivals` gets renamed rather than let
   594	the ambiguity settle in by default.
   595	
   596	Two places where the vocabulary discipline changed a design choice, not just
   597	a name:
   598	
   599	- The resume mark is `arrival_*`, not a rename of `jsonl_*`. Renaming would
   600	  have carried the counts along (§3.2); starting from the arrival axis showed
   601	  the counts dissolve.
   602	- I did not give the arrival log a "mode" or a "canonical vs derived" flag.
   603	  There is no mode. The arrival log is custody and everything else is a
   604	  projection, structurally, with no configuration that could say otherwise.
   605	
   606	---
   607	
   608	## 5. Open questions for Kyle
   609	
   610	Three. Everything else in this document is decided with rationale above.
   611	
   612	**1. Does the signature cover the coordinate?**
   613	
   614	*Recommendation: no — keep `sig` content-only, exactly as today.*
   615	
   616	The existing content commitment deliberately excludes custody context so that
   617	signatures survive transport between stores (`sqlite_store.py:254-273`).
   618	Making the signature cover `(lin, ord)` would mean a record's signature stops
   619	verifying the moment the record is carried into another store at a different
   620	ordinal, which trades away a property that was bought on purpose. Under my
   621	recommendation the coordinate is still tamper-evident via `rh` and still
   622	bindable by slice D via `prev`; it is just not *authored*. The counter-case
   623	is real though: if you want a signer to be able to attest "I placed this
   624	here, at this position", only a coordinate-covering signature says that. That
   625	is a question about what authorship means in the arrival model, which is
   626	yours.
   627	
   628	Either/or: **(a)** `sig` covers content only, coordinate integrity via
   629	`rh`/`prev` [recommended]; **(b)** `sig` covers content *and* coordinate,
   630	accepting that signatures are store-local.
   631	
   632	**2. What does the genesis `body` carry in slice 0?**
   633	
   634	*Recommendation: the minimum — protocol version and the lineage claim — with
   635	the declaration document set deferred to slice A.*
   636	
   637	The existing `absorb_genesis` payload carries `protocol`, `documents`, and
   638	two era pins (`chain_head`, `fact_cursor`) (`sqlite_store.py:794-954`;
   639	`libs/lang/src/lang/document.py:817-826`). The pins exist to make
   640	"everything before me predates historization" verifiable — a statement about
   641	a *pre-existing* store, which a fresh arrival log does not have. Carrying
   642	them into slice 0 would be carrying a solution to a problem the new substrate
   643	does not have yet; they belong in slice A's migration path, where a genesis
   644	really does have history behind it. But genesis is signed and immutable, so
   645	what it carries is not cheaply revisable, and you may prefer to settle its
   646	full shape once.
   647	
   648	Either/or: **(a)** minimal genesis body now, document set and any migration
   649	pins added by slice A [recommended]; **(b)** settle the full genesis body now,
   650	including the document set, so genesis is designed once.
   651	
   652	**3. Ratify `.arrival`, or name it yourself?**
   653	
   654	The suffix is the most visible and least reversible naming decision in the
   655	format, and my rationale in §1 is a preference argument, not a proof. If you
   656	have a name in mind, this is the moment.
   657	
   658	---
   659	
   660	## 6. Summary of what slice 0 must build
   661	
   662	- `<name>.arrival`, `<name>.arrival.lock`, `<name>.arrival.tmp` path
   663	  arithmetic; a new arm in `probe.py`'s suffix dispatch.
   664	- The record encoder/decoder: §2.2 field order, `ensure_ascii`/`allow_nan`/
   665	  `separators` discipline, `rh` over JCS, both directions held to one
   666	  validator (the existing codec's both-directions rule,
   667	  `jsonl_codec.py:29-33`, is the right posture and should be kept).
   668	- Genesis: `O_EXCL` staging, sign-the-final-body, atomic rename, directory
   669	  fsync.
   670	- Append under `flock`: read tail, validate `ord`/`lin`/`prev`, write, fsync.
