cause it reviewed a first draft; cut A's r1 found zero because the gate loop ran first. Sequence gates before cross-family review; do not read zero findings as reviewer weakness without checking what preceded it.\n\n4. THE MAIN-CHECKOUT FULL-SUITE RUN EARNED ITS LOAD-BEARING BILLING AGAIN: surfaced Rule 17's environment-dependence (globs gitignored local docs; green in every worktree and CI, red only where the scratch history lives — finding:rule17-scans-untracked-docs, fix shape git ls-files).\n\n5. ARBITER OVERRULE OF ITS OWN APPLY LIST (the gap-branch assert): a design that promises refuse-or-consume on legal interleave cannot carry a crash on a reachable interleave, however exotic; the multi-process gate is the license that makes 'exotic' insufficient grounds.\n\n6. SUBAGENT MODEL ROUTING was invisible until Kyle asked: every Claude subagent had inherited the arbiter's Fable. Now ruled + codified in both pipeline skills + memory: explicit model per launch, opus for impl/gate/apply, sonnet for exploration.\n\nReceipts: task/arrival-slice-A @ 01M0B8HXRV (full loop), docs/scratch/arrival-sliceA/ (all ledgers, briefs, reports, the failed sol attempt), decision:design/arrival-sliceA-authority @ 01M0B0Z14T.
