    80	---
    81	
    82	## 2. The contract decision (RATIFIED) — what an adoption genesis carries when the store has history
    83	
    84	### 2.1 The deferral, restated exactly
    85	
    86	Kyle's ruling 2 in `01M09GNGW7VTSKDPYMJ027HEPR`:
    87	
    88	> (2) Genesis body MINIMAL in slice 0 — protocol version + lineage claim;
    89	> document set and migration pins are slice A's, where a genesis has real
    90	> history behind it.
    91	
    92	Slice 0 built exactly that. `ArrivalLog.mint` assembles
    93	`body = {"protocol": GRAMMAR_VERSION, "lineage": lin}` (`arrival.py:659`),
    94	signs the content commitment over it (`arrival.py:661`, envelope defined at
    95	`arrival.py:434-448`), and refuses an unsigned genesis (`arrival.py:662-666`).
    96	`_placement_fault` pins two of those properties permanently: a genesis must
    97	carry a signature (`arrival.py:379-383`) and must be **self-naming** —
    98	`body["lineage"]` must equal the record's `lin` (`arrival.py:384-389`). Any
    99	body this document proposes therefore keeps a `lineage` key with that exact
   100	meaning; that field is no longer negotiable.
   101	
   102	### 2.2 Two genesis notions, and why the question is smaller than it looks
   103	
   104	Cut A has to be precise about a word that currently names two different things.
   105	
   106	**Arrival genesis** — the record at ordinal 0. It opens the *arrival lineage*
   107	and is the store's identity. It is structural: position 0 cannot be forged
   108	into existence by appending, which is why the old singleton-inference hijack
   109	vector dissolves rather than being defended against (`arrival.py:714-722`,
   110	carrying `sqlite_store.py:814-822` forward).
   111	
   112	**Declaration genesis** — the `_decl.genesis` fact `absorb_genesis` appends
   113	(`sqlite_store.py:794-954`). It opens the *declaration lineage* and carries
   114	`{protocol, documents, chain_head, fact_cursor}` (`sqlite_store.py:885-890`).
   115	
   116	For a fresh store these coincide, and `01M08N9H8GT4WQXMPGJN3ADWW4` already
   117	rules how: three movements — **self-certifying genesis, declaration absorption,
   118	seal**. Line 1 carries "lineage id, format-version, and the founding PUBLIC
   119	KEY, self-signed by that key"; *then* the declaration batch, signed by the
   120	now-introduced key; then the seal. So the ratified bootstrap has **already
   121	answered the document-set half of ruling 2**: the document set is movement 2,
   122	records at ordinal ≥ 1. It is not genesis body content, and cut A must not put
   123	it there.
   124	
   125	What is left genuinely open is therefore two things, not three:
   126	
   127	1. Does the founding public key go into the genesis body — the movement-1 half
   128	   that slice 0 did **not** build?
   129	2. What happens to the two era pins (`chain_head`, `fact_cursor`) when a
   130	   declaration genesis lands at ordinal N with real history behind it?
   131	
   132	### 2.3 Recommendation — **RATIFIED** (Kyle, 2026-08-18)
   133	
   134	**Adoption genesis body = `{protocol, lineage, key}`. Nothing else. The era
   135	pins dissolve; the document set stays in movement 2; containment pins never
   136	appear in a live-store genesis at all.**
   137	
   138	Ruled as proposed. Slice 0's minimal body was slice-0-scoped, not a ceiling —
   139	the bootstrap fact had already ruled the destination, and cut A is the slice
   140	that arrives at it.
   141	
   142	Concretely, three statements:
   143	
   144	**(a) `key` joins the genesis body.** The founding public key, in the inline
   145	registry format `sign.ed25519` already defines (`libs/sign/src/sign/ed25519.py:63,74`
   146	— raw-32-byte base64, copy-identical to the on-disk `ed25519.pub`).
   147	
   148	**The primary reason is that it is already ruled.** Movement 1 of
   149	`01M08N9H8GT4WQXMPGJN3ADWW4` says line 1 "carries lineage id, format-version,
   150	and the founding PUBLIC KEY, self-signed by that key — the TOFU anchor at
   151	ordinal 0". Slice 0 did not build it: `arrival.py:659` writes two fields, and
   152	the module contains no verifier at all. Cut A is the slice where lineage genesis
   153	moves into arrival, so it is the slice that owes movement 1. Adding `key` also
   154	makes the ratified verifier rule statable in one clause — a key is valid at
   155	position N iff introduced at position < N, or N is 0 and the record is
   156	self-certifying; self-certification legal at ordinal 0 and nowhere else.
   157	
   158	Supporting, not load-bearing: it is also what makes cut A's own scope sentence
   159	true. "Records become complete", and completeness item 3 in the ratified grammar
   160	is "who says it … verifiable against the observer registry **without consulting
   161	any projection**" (design doc §2.9). Note the limit of that argument, so it is
   162	not leaned on harder than it holds: the `.vertex` observer-key registry is
   163	**not** a projection, so registry-anchored verification does survive deleting
   164	every projection. What an in-log key buys is that attribution stops depending on
   165	an artifact outside the log at all — which is the stronger reading of "answers
   166	from arrival", and the reading §7 G1 adopts.
   167	
   168	**(b) `fact_cursor` dissolves.** Today the pin exists so that "everything before
   169	me predates historization" is verifiable (`sqlite_store.py:823-825`); it is the
   170	newest fact by rowid (`sqlite_store.py:880-883`). Under arrival that statement
   171	is `ord < N`, structurally, because ordinals are dense and gapless. This is the
   172	same dissolution the ratified grammar already took on the resume mark, where
   173	counts custody dissolved for the identical reason (design doc §3.2, "the ordinal
   174	is dense, so 'how many records precede this one' is `ord`, by construction").
   175	Keeping the pin would re-import a custody field the substrate has already
   176	retired.
   177	
   178	**(c) `chain_head` does not go in.** It is the latest chained tick's row hash —
   179	a value computed over sqlite tick rows (`sqlite_store.py:879`,
   180	`current_chain_head`). Putting it inside a signed, immutable arrival record
   181	makes a permanent integrity claim rest on a projection artifact, which is the
   182	first prohibition of Law 1: "any local semantic or integrity claim resting on an
   183	artifact other than arrival". If cut D wants a tick-chain anchor in a
   184	declaration genesis, it names an arrival coordinate `(lin, ord)`, not a tick row
   185	hash. That is cut D's to design; cut A's obligation is to not write the
   186	projection-shaped pin into an immutable record first.
   187	
   188	### 2.4 Alternatives considered
   189	
   190	**A1 — carry the legacy pins forward verbatim** (`chain_head` + `fact_cursor`
   191	into the arrival-era declaration genesis body). Rejected on (b) and (c): one pin
   192	is structurally redundant, the other names a projection. It also has the shape
   193	`observation:pattern/overgeneralization-returns-renamed` describes — the retired
   194	concept surviving as a field name because it was in the previous payload.
   195	
   196	**A2 — settle the whole genesis body now, document set included, at ordinal 0.**
   197	Rejected: it contradicts the ratified three movements, which put declaration
   198	absorption at ordinal ≥ 1 with a signature from the *now-introduced* key. It
   199	also forces every store to possess its declaration at mint time, which
   200	`absorb_genesis` explicitly does not require — it is a ceremony run on a store
   201	that already has facts.
   202	
   203	**A3 — minimal body unchanged (`{protocol, lineage}`), founding key deferred.**
   204	This is the smallest cut A and it is tempting. Note precisely what it does and
   205	does not cost, because it is easy to overstate: records are **signed either
   206	way**. `mint` refuses an unsigned genesis (`arrival.py:662-666`) and
   207	`_placement_fault` pins that permanently (`arrival.py:379-383`), so under A3
   208	there is no unsigned era and no attribution gap. The cost is **key locality**:
   209	the verifying key for the log's earliest records lives only in the `.vertex`,
   210	so the log's attribution of its own genesis rests permanently on an artifact
   211	outside it. And that dependency can never be closed retroactively — a later
   212	in-log key introduction is an append at ordinal > 0, which under the ratified
   213	verifier rule can never be self-certifying. Rejected on that permanence, and on
   214	§2.3a: movement 1 is ruled, and cut A is the slice that owes it.
   215	
   216	**A4 — key in the body, but also a coordinate-covering signature** so genesis
   217	attests its own position. Rejected: Kyle's ruling 1 in
   218	`01M09GNGW7VTSKDPYMJ027HEPR` already fixed `sig` as content-only, and
   219	`arrival.py:434-448` implements it. The coordinate stays tamper-evident through
   220	`rh`.
   221	
   222	### 2.5 What this changes in code
   223	
   224	- `ArrivalLog.mint` gains a required `key` argument and writes
   225	  `{"protocol", "lineage", "key"}` (`arrival.py:658-672`). The signature
   226	  envelope is unchanged — the key rides inside `body`, which the commitment
   227	  already covers (`arrival.py:447`).
   228	
   229	  *Required* — for cut A's ceremonies, which always have a signer. That is the
   230	  whole of what this bullet rules, and the scope limit is deliberate.
   231	
   232	  **NAMED OPEN ITEM, deferred to the sidecar slice: the keyless-genesis
   233	  collision.** `01M08N9H8GT4WQXMPGJN3ADWW4` also rules a keyless variant —
   234	  "same line 1, no key field, unsigned, classification era UNSIGNED; a later
   235	  introduction opens the signed era" — and cut A takes **no position** on it
   236	  (Kyle, 2026-08-18). This document does not declare
   237	  `01M09GNGW7VTSKDPYMJ027HEPR` to supersede that clause. The collision is real
   238	  and has two halves worth stating so the sidecar slice inherits the trade
   239	  rather than rediscovering it:
   240	
   241	  - At HEAD an unsigned genesis is already unspellable — `mint` refuses one
   242	    (`arrival.py:662-666`) and `_placement_fault` pins the refusal
   243	    (`arrival.py:379-383`) — so the ruled keyless path has no way to exist
   244	    under the current grammar.
   245	  - A `key` field inside the hashed body is not a free addition on the
   246	    migration path: it would break same-lineage determinism, the property that
   247	    two keyless custodians re-encoding the same source arrive at the same
   248	    lineage. And a keyless custodian has nothing to sign with in the first
   249	    place.
   250	
   251	  The sidecar slice resolves this as one of two deliberate acts — a grammar
   252	  amendment (a carve-out or a version bump) or a deliberate retirement of
   253	  keyless migration — with the trade visible either way. Kyle's ruling then.
   254	  Nothing in cut A forces the question.
   255	- `_placement_fault`'s ordinal-0 arm gains one clause: genesis must carry a
   256	  well-formed `body["key"]`, and the genesis signature must verify **against
   257	  that key** (`arrival.py:372-390`). That last clause is what makes the genesis
   258	  self-certifying rather than merely self-naming, and it is the first
   259	  verification `arrival.py` performs at all.
   260	- A verifier is **injected, never imported**, matching the `Signer` posture
   600	(Q3, §8), so if any other cut-A surface wants it closed, that is a new question
   601	put to Kyle, not a design choice made inside an implementation.
   602	
   603	**C3. Reads execute through projections; that is not a violation.** Law 1
   604	permits it in as many words. Cut A's read path is unchanged, and a reviewer who
   605	reads `resolve_store_path` still returning a `.db` should read §4.1 before
   606	filing it.
   607	
   608	---
   609	
   610	## 7. The gate
   611	
   612	Verbatim from `plan:arrival-libs-slice-A` @ `01M090QW6ZHDMPDP6P5TZAYTPB`:
   613	
   614	> GATE: authority test answered literally — delete every projection, everything
   615	> still answers from arrival; plus libs/sign + observer-key registry
   616	> file-position trace.
   617	
   618	Plus the per-slice law from the same fact: "apps/ diff-empty is a per-slice gate
   619	check."
   620	
   621	**G1 — the authority test, literally.** Build a store, exercise it (facts,
   622	ticks, a declaration absorb, a seal), then delete every projection — the `.db`
   623	and its `-wal`/`-shm` siblings (`residence.py:97-105`) and any `.jsonl` — and
   624	assert that identity, records, authorship and ordering all still answer from the
   625	`.arrival` file alone. The walk is `ArrivalLog.walk()` (C2). "Everything"
   626	includes the store's own lineage, which under §4.3 is the single strongest
   627	assertion in the gate: with `store_meta` gone, `own_lineage` must still be
   628	derivable.
   629	
   630	**G2 — the libs/sign + observer-key registry file-position trace.** **RULED**
   631	(Kyle, 2026-08-18): the clause is the **exclusivity claim**, not a survey.
   632	
   633	*The claim.* The arrival verifier cut A builds resolves every verifying key
   634	from `(lineage, ordinal)` coordinates **in the log only**. No libs code
   635	consults the `.vertex` for an arrival key. `sign.ed25519` supplies the key
   636	format (`libs/sign/src/sign/ed25519.py:17,63,74,82` — raw-32-byte base64,
   637	copy-identical to the on-disk `ed25519.pub`); it does not supply the key's
   638	*location*, because under §2.3a the location is a coordinate.
   639	
   640	*The trace.* Produce `(key, lineage, ordinal)` rows **with every projection
   641	deleted**, after introducing a second key at a stated ordinal. Two keys, not
   642	one: a single founding key at ordinal 0 cannot distinguish "resolved from the
   643	log" from "resolved from anywhere and happened to match", whereas a second
   644	introduction at ordinal M exercises the ruled verifier clause — valid at N iff
   645	introduced at N' < N — and the trace must show the ordinal it was introduced
   646	at.
   647	
   648	*The instantiation boundary, and it is load-bearing.* The **existing
   649	tick-chain verifier** that apps/loops composes from the `.vertex` `observers{}`
   650	registry is **untouched this slice**. It is projection-era machinery
   651	(`sqlite_store.py:1755-1765` describes the injected verifier; `:1767-1773`
   652	names the `.vertex` anchor), and apps/ diff-empty is law (G3). The `observers{}`
   653	block is **demoted, not deleted**: it stops being "the anchor" and becomes a
   654	legacy projection-era artifact, with retirement scheduled at the surface cut /
   655	CLI rebuild. A cut-A reviewer who finds `.vertex`-sourced keys still in use by
   656	the tick path should read this paragraph before filing it; a cut-A reviewer who
   657	finds them in the **arrival** verifier has found a real defect.
   658	
   659	*Total-rewrite detection, restated as a distribution property.* The known scope
   660	boundary at `sqlite_store.py:1767-1773` — a total rewrite renders as a clean
   661	pre-signature store — does not become worse under an in-log registry, and the
   662	honest statement of the defence changes shape. A fresh mint diverges in **every
   663	`rh` from ordinal 0** against any copy: a peer, a backup, git history, or an
   664	aggregator's lineage-id membership pin. That divergence is what makes
   665	self-containment worth having. A **local, owner-level rewrite is accepted as
   666	undefendable by any local artifact** — the `.vertex` included, since an
   667	attacker who can rewrite the store can rewrite the `.vertex` beside it. So
   668	moving keys into the log costs nothing here and buys the distribution check.
   669	`horizon`/`potential` are explicitly out of scope for this gate.
   670	
   671	**G3 — apps/ diff-empty.** `git diff --stat main -- apps/` is empty at the end
   672	of the cut. Held by construction per §4.1/§4.4, and it is also the reason for
   673	the `late_arrivals` KEEP (§5.1).
   674	
   675	---
   676	
   677	## 8. The rulings that closed the open questions
   678	
   679	All three were ruled by Kyle on 2026-08-18, in-session with Fable. They are
   680	recorded here in the form they were asked so the question and its answer stay
   681	adjacent; the sections they govern carry the same rulings inline.
   682	
   683	**Q1 — Does the founding public key go into the genesis body in cut A?**
   684	**RATIFIED: yes.** Adoption genesis body is `{protocol, lineage, key}`, the key
   685	self-signed at ordinal 0 per movement 1 of `01M08N9H8GT4WQXMPGJN3ADWW4`, and cut
   686	A builds `arrival.py`'s first verifier implementing the ruled clause — a key is
   687	valid at position N iff introduced at position < N, or N is the genesis position
   688	and the record is self-certifying. The reasoning that settled it: slice 0's
   689	minimal body was slice-0-scoped, while the bootstrap fact had already ruled the
   690	destination, so this is arrival rather than addition. Instantiated at §2.3a and
   691	§2.5.
   692	
   693	**Q2 — What exactly does "libs/sign + observer-key registry file-position trace"
   694	require?** **RATIFIED: the exclusivity claim**, with the instantiation boundary
   695	that leaves the existing `.vertex`-composed tick-chain verifier untouched and
   696	demotes the `observers{}` block from anchor to legacy projection-era artifact
   697	(retirement scheduled at the surface cut / CLI rebuild). Total-rewrite detection
   698	is restated as a distribution property; a local owner-level rewrite is accepted
   699	as undefendable by any local artifact. Written out in ruled form at §7 G2.
   700	
   701	**Q3 — Do the three arbiter rulings from slice 0 stand?** **DISCHARGED: all
   702	three confirmed** — multi-word denylist terms as identifier bans, the
   703	sense-qualified bans deliberately not mechanized, and the O(n) residual accepted
   704	at its stated width (`01M09NZKTC2EE13W5FGQ6PDNYB`). §4.5 and C2 therefore stand
   705	on ruled ground, not on overturnable overnight rulings.
   706	
   707	**One item is deferred by name, not answered: the keyless-genesis collision**
   708	(§2.5). Cut A takes no position; the sidecar slice resolves it as either a
   709	deliberate grammar amendment or a deliberate retirement of keyless migration.
   710	Nothing in cut A forces the question, because cut A's live ceremonies always
   711	have a signer.
   712	
   713	---
   714	
   715	## 9. What cut A builds, in order
   716	
   717	1. `probe.py`'s `.arrival` arm and the half-migrated-store classification
   718	   (§4.2) — ruled first, and everything else asks it questions.
   719	2. `residence.canonical_mode`, the deletion of `is_jsonl_canonical`, the
   720	   `log_path_for` replacement, the re-exported path helpers (§4.1).
   721	3. The genesis body extension and the ordinal-0 verifier clause (§2.5) — Q1
   722	   ratified, no longer gated.
   723	4. The two writer ceremonies' re-point, arrival mode only (§4.3), and
   724	   `adopt_lineage`'s refusal (§5.2).
   725	5. The libs-side sweep against the plan's census, resume mark on the ratified
   726	   three fields (§4.4).
   727	6. Rule 18 `_SCAN_TARGETS` += `residence.py`, `probe.py`; Rule 17 allowlist
   728	   shrink as the sweep forces it (§4.5).
   729	7. The `late_arrivals` docstring sense note (§5.1).
   730	8. The gate: G1, G2, G3 (§7).

