Rule17 current docs debt tail
     1	# Fold replay order is receipt order
     2	
     3	**Status:** ratified 2026-08-15. Supersedes `decision:design/replay-total-order`.
     4	**Fact:** `decision:design/replay-receipt-order`.
     5	**Anchor:** `docs/CONCEPTUAL_MODEL.md` — *"Event time never substitutes for
     6	receipt order."* This change is that line finally holding everywhere.
     7	
     8	## The decision
     9	
    10	A vertex store replays its facts in **receipt order** — `facts.rowid`, the order
    11	this store actually received them. That is the fold. The ULID `id` is stable
    12	identity; `ts` is event-time metadata the emitter supplied. Neither orders the
    13	fold.
    14	
    15	`(ts, id)` survives as an explicit **read lens**: an event-time projection a
    16	reader may ask for. It is a way of *looking at* the facts, never the order they
    17	are folded in.
    18	
    19	Receipt order is **store-local**. A rowid means something only relative to the
    20	store that assigned it. Everything below follows from that one property.
    21	
    22	### What it replaces
    23	
    24	`decision:design/replay-total-order` made `(ts, id)` the fold order on the
    25	grounds that it is a store-independent total order, so any two stores holding
    26	the same facts fold to the same state. That was true and it was expensive: a
    27	backdated arrival lands in the middle of the history, so every append could
    28	re-order the whole fold, and the only sound way to answer a read was to re-fold
    29	everything. Ingest was O(n²) in consequence.
    30	
    31	It was also dishonest in a way the conceptual model already named. Event time is
    32	a claim the emitter makes; receipt order is what this store witnessed. Folding on
    33	the former let an emitter's clock rewrite what a reader had already been shown.
    34	
    35	Under receipt order a fact folds where it arrived, an append is a suffix, and a
    36	held fold can be advanced by folding just the new rows onto it. Determinism is
    37	unchanged — it is now determinism *given a store*, which is the honest scope.
    38	
    39	## Rulings
    40	
    41	### R1 — DISCARDED at the arrival cut B
    42	
    43	R1 ruled that `store.merge_store` inserted the source's rows ordered by
    44	`(ts, id)`, and that the rowids the merge handed out *were* the merged store's
    45	fold order — the merge being the receipt event.
    46	
    47	**That doctrine is discarded** (`decision:design/arrival-sliceB-projections`).
    48	A merge into an arrival-canonical store no longer inserts into sqlite at all:
    49	it appends records into the target's arrival log, and the index is re-derived
    50	from that log. The merged store's ordering claim is **the target's arrival
    51	ordinal — the order records arrived at the target — and nothing else**. No
    52	claim is made about event time, about rowids as a primitive, or about the
    53	relationship between `merge(A,B)` and `merge(B,A)`.
    54	
    55	What survives, unchanged in substance:
    56	
    57	- **Determinism per direction.** For given source content, `merge(A,B)` lays
    58	  down one specific ordinal sequence, every time — never a scan order.
    59	- **Content equality across directions.** Both results hold the same row set
    60	  with the same ids (dedup is on the id primary key). They disagree on
    61	  sequence, not on what was merged. The two merges *are* different custody
    62	  events, and the ordinal says so.
    63	
    64	Code and prose must claim determinism and content equality — never identical
    65	fold sequences. The sqlite-canonical arm of `merge_store` is untouched by the
    66	arrival cut and keeps its insertion SQL; what changed is that the SQL no
    67	longer carries a doctrine.
    68	
    69	### R2 — combined reads use the `(ts, id)` lens (named interim state)
    70	
    71	A combine/discover vertex reads across several attached member stores. rowid is
    72	per-store, so a `UNION ALL` over members has **no receipt axis at all** —
    73	`ORDER BY rowid` across it would be meaningless, not merely different.
    74	
    75	Combined reads therefore fall back to the explicit `(ts, id)` read lens
    76	(`libs/engine/src/engine/vertex_reader.py`, `_combined_read` and
    77	`facts_in_range`). A combined fold is a **lens projection, not a receipt
    78	replay**, and it may disagree with a single-store fold over the same facts.
    79	
    80	This is an **interim state, named rather than hidden**. What receipt order means
    81	across members — a per-member cursor vector, a merge-into-one ceremony, or
    82	accepting that aggregates fold on a lens — is an open design question. Until it
    83	is ruled, no code should claim single-store and combined folds agree.
    84	
    85	### R3 — the CAS token rides the store's own receipt axis
    86	
    87	`SqliteStore.declaration_head` / `_declaration_head_in_txn` surface the token
    88	`absorb_edit` compares `expected_head` against. The token and the resolver must
    89	move together or optimistic concurrency compares two different notions of
    90	"head".
    91	
    92	R3's substance is that the token rides **the receipt axis of the store that
    93	issues it**. What that axis *is* depends on the family, and since the arrival
    94	cut the two families answer differently
    95	(`decision:design/arrival-sliceC-ordering`):
    96	
    97	- **sqlite / JSONL-canonical** — `(rowid, id)`, byte-for-byte as R3 first
    98	  ruled. These are frozen legacy families.
    99	- **arrival-canonical** — `(record_ordinal, id)`, the coordinate of the newest
   100	  self-lineage declaration on the arrival axis, answered by walking the log.
   101	  `ArrivalStore` overrides `_declaration_head_in_txn` for it. A batch record's
   102	  rows share one ordinal, so the fact id is doing real tie-break work here.
   103	
   104	Both shapes are `[int, str]`, so a persisted v1 rowid coordinate is
   105	indistinguishable from a v2 ordinal by inspection. The intent version is the
   106	only discriminator, which is why it bumped rather than the coordinate being
   107	re-read optimistically.
   108	
   109	The `at` (`rowid <=`) / `as_of` (`ts <=`) **selector duality** is untouched by
   110	all of this. Both are selectors, and neither is a lens — see the ruling below.
   111	
   112	### `as_of` is a selector, not a lens
   113	
   114	`decision:design/as-of-is-a-selector-not-a-lens`.
   115	
   116	`as_of` is an **event-time selector over the receipt-order fold**. It decides
   117	WHICH facts fold — `ts <= T` — and never how they order. The rows it selects
   118	fold in receipt order, exactly like every other fold; `at` differs from it only
   119	in where the cutoff is drawn (`rowid <=` versus `ts <=`), not in what happens
   120	after.
   121	
   122	The code was already right: `StoreReader.facts_by_kind` applies `until_ts` as a
   123	`WHERE` clause and orders by `rowid` regardless. It was this record that
   124	overclaimed, by filing `as_of` under the read lens.
   125	
   126	The distinction matters because it is the whole point of the change. A lens
   127	re-**orders**; a selector re-**scopes**. Calling `as_of` a lens smuggles the
   128	superseded model back in — it implies that asking an event-time question gets
   129	you an event-time *ordering*, which is exactly what receipt order removed. A
   130	backdated fact inside the cutoff folds at its receipt position, last, the same
   131	as it would at head. Pinned by
   132	`libs/engine/tests/test_fold_as_of.py::TestAsOfFoldsInReceiptOrder`.
   133	
   134	Event-time **ordering** lives on the lens surface only — the `(ts, id)` read
   135	lens, timelines, combined reads.
   136	
   137	## What follows for prose
   138	
   139	The load-bearing distinction, and the one the ratchet enforces:
   140	
   141	- **Fold / replay order** — receipt order, `rowid`, store-local. Never `(ts, id)`.
   142	- **Selector** — `at` (`rowid <=`) and `as_of` / `until_ts` (`ts <=`). These
   143	  choose which facts fold. They do not order anything; what they select folds
   144	  in receipt order. Never call a selector a lens.
   145	- **Read lens** — `(ts, id)`, timelines, combined reads. This is the only place
   146	  event-time ORDERING lives. Explicitly a projection. Must say so.
   147	
   148	"Late arrival" is now a **lens-level observation**, not a fold warning. A fact
   149	that arrives out of event-time order folds last, like any other arrival, and
   150	perturbs nothing. What it tells a reader is that the `(ts, id)` lens over that
   151	window will order those rows differently from the fold — so a lens-ordered view
   152	of the window is not the fold's history.
   153	
   154	`tests/architecture/test_rule_17_fold_order_prose_is_receipt_order.py` is the
   155	ratchet: no shipped prose in `libs/`, `apps/`, `spec/`, or `docs/` may claim
   156	`(ts, id)` is the fold order outside a shrink-only allowlist of genuinely
   157	lens-labeled sites.
   158	
   159	## Where it is implemented
   160	
   161	| Concern | Site |
   162	|---|---|
   163	| Kind-filtered fold read | `libs/engine/src/engine/store_reader.py` — `facts_by_kind` |
   164	| Raw / cursor reads | `libs/engine/src/engine/sqlite_store.py` — `since`, `since_raw`, `replay_cursor` |
   165	| Single-store vertex fold | `libs/engine/src/engine/vertex_reader.py` — `_combined_read` (single-store branch) |
   166	| Declaration resolution + head | `libs/engine/src/engine/declaration.py` |
   167	| CAS token (R3), legacy families | `libs/engine/src/engine/sqlite_store.py` — `declaration_head` |
   168	| CAS token (R3), arrival axis | `libs/engine/src/engine/arrival_store.py` — `_declaration_head_in_txn` |
   169	| Declared-ordering primitive | `libs/atoms/src/atoms/ordering.py` — `Arrival` / `ByKey` / `totalize` |
   170	| Declared read ordering | `libs/engine/src/engine/store_reader.py` — `ordered` (prefix SELECTS, the declared key ORDERS) |
   171	| Incremental fold | `libs/engine/src/engine/handle.py` — `replay_mode="checkpoint-suffix"` |
   172	| Suffix fold primitive | `libs/atoms/src/atoms/spec.py` — `Spec.replay_from` |
   173	| Combined-read lens (R2) | `libs/engine/src/engine/vertex_reader.py` |
   174	| Normative spec | `spec/conformance/SCHEMA.md` §6 replay, §7 witness, §8 merge, §9 lens |
   175	| Lens conformance vectors | `spec/conformance/vectors/lens/` |
   176	
   177	## Open
   178	
   179	- **R2's interim state.** Combined folds ride a lens. Needs a ruling.
   180	- The merge vector still carries the name
   181	  `merge-interleaved-timestamps-replay-total-order`, from the superseded
   182	  decision. Renaming it regenerates a conformance vector, so it is deliberately
   183	  left for a spec change rather than a prose sweep.

