    88	## 3The collapse
    89	
    90	The store is what this custodian received. Everything else is a question asked of it.
    91	
    92	 The canonical store becomes an append-only, lineage-local arrival log. It holds
    93	 complete canonical records plus a minted lineage genesis — the
    94	 custodian’s own identity marker. Its position is the witness position:
    95	 (arrival_lineage, ordinal). It is never merged, never rebuilt, never rewound.
    96	
    97	 Everything else is a projection of an arrival prefix: the sqlite index,
    98	 full-text search, fold state, graph views, checkpoints — and, crucially, the
    99	 JSONL file itself — today’s authoritative artifact, intended to be tracked
   100	 and shared through Git though that tracking was never actually realized — which becomes a deterministic, canonically-ordered
   101	 synchronization projection. Authority inverts: the file people diff, review, and share stops
   102	 being the store and becomes a derived representation of it.
   103	
   104	 [figure omitted]
   105	
   106	 The authority inversion. Today git merges write into the canonical artifact, and
   107	 custody order is whatever line order results. Under arrival, exactly one artifact is
   108	 canonical and git only ever touches a derived projection; the single write path into custody
   109	 is the custodian’s own admission step.
   110	
   111	### Three clocks, three authorities
   112	
   113	 The model keeps a strict ontology of time, and it survived every pass unchanged. Each temporal
   114	 datum has exactly one authority, and no datum ever substitutes for another:
   115	
   116	 | Clock | Field | Authority | What it means
   117	
   118	 | Claim | fact.ts | Claimant | “This is when I say the thing happened.” Observer-asserted event time. Data, never substrate.
   119	
   120	 | Emission | fact.id | Emitter | Identity and dedup (a ULID). Totalizes orderings deterministically; carries no time authority.
   121	
   122	 | Witness | (lineage, ordinal) | Custodian | “This is when I received it, relative to everything else I received.” Order, not wall-clock.
   123	
   124	 Wall-clock at the custody layer exists in exactly one place: a seal. A tick
   125	 binds its own signed timestamp to a witness position, so “this store had received X by
   126	 time T” is an attested claim with a chain behind it — not a bare field
   127	 anyone could have written. (Section 7 covers why the model deliberately adds no per-record
   128	 receipt timestamp.)
   129	
   130	## 4The five laws
   131	
   132	 The smallest attackable surface. Everything else in the record is derivable from these;
   133	 break one and you break the architecture.
   134	
   135	Law 1
   136	
   137	The store is the arrival log. Projections compute answers; arrival determines what those answers are about.
   138	
   139	FORBIDS — any local semantic or integrity claim resting on an artifact other than arrival. Reads may execute through sqlite, FTS, or a checkpoint — that is what they are for. If a projection disagrees with arrival, the projection is stale or invalid, never the other way around.
   140	
   141	Law 2
   142	
   143	Ordering over an arrival basis is witness order or a declared (thing, id) order. id totalizes and carries no domain-time authority.
   144	
   145	FORBIDS — the substrate learning what ts, fiction_time, or priority mean; and id being read as a clock. The second prohibition matters more after the generalization, because most declared keys are not time at all.
   146	
   147	Law 3
   148	
   149	Drivers propose candidates; only the custodian admits records and mints witness positions.
   150	
   151	FORBIDS — two things minting positions. A driver that can append to arrival is a second custodian, and the single-custodian invariant is the one property arrival exists to hold. The inbound interface is deliberately weak: it returns candidates, it does not write.
   152	
   153	Law 4
   154	
   155	Witness positions compare only within one arrival lineage.
   156	
   157	FORBIDS — cross-lineage comparison answering at all. It is not false, it is undefined, and it must fail structurally rather than return a confident wrong answer. Copying arrival bytes for backup or replication is legal and recommended; the typed error is opening a copied arrival log as another custodian while silently claiming continuation of the lineage. Restore is an explicit lineage act, never a silent one.
   158	
   159	Law 5
   160	
   161	Projection maintenance is derived from ordering semantics and reduction algebra. It is never an independent store mode.
   162	
   163	FORBIDS — a freely-declared maintenance class. A reduction wrongly marked order-independent produces silently wrong fold state — the only silent failure shape this architecture permits, so the claim is checked, not trusted (§5).
   164	
   165	 The laws were frozen with a standing reopening bar, so future review needs
   166	 evidence rather than aesthetics:
   167	
   168	Falsification criterion
   169	
   170	 Show a required Loops operation that cannot be expressed as custody in one lineage
   171	 plus declared interpretation/projection over an arrival basis, without introducing
   172	 false historical claims. Five review passes produced none.
   180	 priority, (chapter, scene) are all peers of ts. So
   181	 “event” leaves the vocabulary entirely and the substrate exposes one read
   182	 primitive:
   183	
   184	ordered(prefix = W, key = K)
   185	 = stable total ordering of arrival[0:W] by (K(record), record.id)
   186	
   187	 A projection declares order arrival or order by KEY. The substrate
   188	 never learns what any key means — domain vocabulary stays in declarations —
   189	 and id serves only as the deterministic tiebreak.
   190	
