   102	
   103	 The argument that decides it beyond reuse: the record-completeness gate makes JSONL-canonical
   104	 records the bindable exchange representation. Custody format equal to exchange format
   105	 means one representation of canon — no translation layer between what a custodian holds
   106	 and what it publishes, no divergence surface between two spellings of the same record. And
   107	 there is a product stance in it: a loops user’s entire evidentiary history is readable
   108	 with cat, greppable, diffable — the same “see it directly”
   109	 property the .vertex fold-out gives declarations, held at the store level.
   110	
   111	Ruled, with two riders
   112	
   113	 The arrival log’s physical format is JSONL — the existing codec.
   114	 Rider one: the genesis fact carries a format-version field, the one-break
   115	 budget’s exit hatch — if a different encoding ever becomes necessary it is a
   116	 future loud break, detectable at line 1 rather than archaeologically. Rider two:
   117	 blobs stay out of the grammar — large binary content is sourced and
   118	 referenced from facts (content-addressed, by hash) when needed, never inline, so attachments
   119	 can never become a format argument later.
   120	
   121	## 3Line grammar and position
   122	
   123	 Three line shapes, discriminated by "t", exactly as the codec defines them today:
   124	
   125	{"t":"fact", id, kind, ts, observer, origin, payload[, signature]}
   126	{"t":"tick", id, name, ts, since, origin, payload,
   127	 prev_hash, window_start, fact_cursor, window_hash[, signature]}
   128	{"t":"batch", rows:[<fact records>]} // ≥2 rows, no nesting, no ticks inside
   129	
   130	 The codec’s disciplines carry over unchanged, because they are precisely arrival’s
   131	 requirements: payload rides as the verbatim stored string and is never
   132	 re-serialized, so round-trips re-derive byte-identical commitment hashes; signature
   133	 is absent, never null, when unsigned — one canonical spelling per record; a
   134	 line is the atomicity unit, so a ceremony is indexed whole or truncated whole; a torn tail is
   135	 truncated, never skipped.
   136	
   137	### Witness position
   138	
   139	 A record’s witness position is (lineage, ordinal) — the lineage
   140	 minted at genesis, the ordinal its position in the record sequence: line order, with
   141	 batch lines expanding in row order. Ordinal is per-record, not per-line
   142	 — forced by the existing machinery, which indexes each batch row to its own rowid, and by
   143	 class (b) migration verification, which recomputes window hashes over the carried order
   144	 and would break if a migrated store’s fact counts disagreed with its own signed history.
   145	 A batch therefore occupies a contiguous ordinal range; atomicity is an
   146	 admission property (the line commits whole or truncates whole), never an addressing
   147	 property — a read basis may land mid-batch, exactly as a fact-id address can name a
   148	 mid-ceremony fact today. The record is what position addresses; the line is the atomicity unit.
   149	
   150	 Position is computed by append(record) → stamp, never
   151	 stored in the record. A stored ordinal would be a second authority that could disagree with
   152	 actual position — the exact defect class merge created — and the migration ruling
   153	 forbids fabricating per-record receipt data for history that never carried it. Under arrival,
   154	 seq:N inverts from the most dangerous address form (a rebuild renames its
   155	 referent) to the canonical coordinate: ordinals in an append-only, never-rebuilt file are
   156	 stable by construction.
   157	
   158	## 4What the log contains
   159	
   160	Everything that is evidence. Exactly four exclusions.
   160	Everything that is evidence. Exactly four exclusions.
   161	
   162	 The conception ratified in-session: a 1.0 arrival log contains the entirety of the
   163	 vertex — genesis, the vertex definition, the observers, the memberships, every assertion,
   164	 every seal. The census walked every artifact class in a complete loops deployment and the
   165	 boundary came out crisp:
   166	
   167	 | 
   168	 | | Artifact | Disposition
   169	
   170	 | in | Assertions, seals | fact and tick lines, as ever
   171	
   172	 | in | Vertex definition (kinds, folds, name) | Already in: _decl.* facts via the absorb ceremonies, as batch lines
   173	
   174	 | in | Observer introductions, grants, rotations, revocations | Declaration-vocabulary facts (§6–7); the observers{} block joins the declaration document set
   175	
   176	 | in | Aggregation membership | Declaration facts in the aggregator’s lineage (§8)
   177	
   178	 | in | Genesis + migration receipts, classification facts | Rebirth template: receipted fact + sealing tick
   179	
   180	 | out | Secrets | Private keys never enter the log
   181	
   182	 | out | Code | Lenses are interpretation machinery, not custody
   183	
   184	 | out | Transport | Driver config, credentials, cursors, locator maps — watermarks, resettable
   185	
   186	 | out | Its own address | A log naming its path lies the moment the file moves
   187	
   188	The .vertex file becomes a projection
   189	
   190	 The editable KDL file is folded out of the arrival log for convenience
   191	 — iterating, experimenting, seeing it directly — and edits-on-file are
   192	 re-absorbed through the declaration ceremony. It is a derived artifact and
   193	 the interface must say so, the same way every file-to-store authority transition is
   194	 disclosed. Derived artifacts never masquerade as canonical ones — including the ones
   195	 people look at most.
   196	
   197	## 5Genesis: three movements
   198	
   199	loops init stops creating files and starts opening a lineage.
   200	
   201	 A fresh 1.0 log opens with a genesis ceremony of three movements — self-certifying
   202	 genesis, declaration absorption, seal — committed before the first ordinary admission.
   203	 The ordering carries a bootstrap proof (caught in Sol’s review of this document’s
   204	 first draft, which had the key introduction after the declarations it was supposed to
   205	 sign): the genesis fact is the founding introduction. Line 1 carries the
   206	 lineage id, the format-version, and the founding public key, self-signed by that key —
   207	 so every subsequent line, the declaration batch included, is signed by a key the log has
   208	 already introduced. No line is unsigned in a signed lineage; no verification is retroactive.
   209	
   210	 [figure omitted]
   211	
   212	 Anatomy of an arrival log. Movements: self-certifying genesis (id + format-version +
   213	 founding key; for migrations, source hashes/counts and classification facts), declaration
   214	 absorption signed by the just-introduced key, then the genesis seal. Ordinary admissions
   215	 follow; the numbers shown are record ordinals, which is why the batch at line six spans two.
   216	
   217	 For a migration, movement one is the legacy-adoption receipt: source hashes and counts on the
   218	 rebirth template, plus the classification facts — order
   219	 verified / asserted / minted
   220	 per segment, signature era orthogonal (§11). One store, one log, one genesis:
   221	 classification annotates segments; it never fragments the lineage.
   222	
   223	## 6Observers, keys, verification
   224	
   225	The registry moves into the log, and verification becomes position-dependent.
   226	
   227	 Today the observer registry — name to public key, grants included — lives in the
   228	 .vertex file, and verify_chain checks all of history against the
   229	 registry’s present state. That timelessness is a defect twice over: rotate a key
   230	 and old signatures break; add a key and it retroactively validates records signed before anyone
   231	 trusted it. And it is the exact failure measured in the homelab: a peer’s ticks
   232	 unverifiable in one vertex because its key lived only in another vertex’s file.
   233	
   234	Position-dependent verification
   235	
   236	 Observer introductions, grants, rotations, and revocations are ordinary signed facts in the
   237	 log. The verifier walks from line 1 maintaining the key registry as a projection:
   238	 a key is valid at position N iff introduced before N and not revoked
   239	 before N — or N is the genesis position and the record is
   240	 self-certifying, the one explicit clause of the bootstrap (§5); legal at
   241	 ordinal 0 and nowhere else, never retroactive. Every lineage carries the keys needed to
   242	 verify itself — the log is cryptographically self-verifying with
   243	 nothing beside it. (Self-verifying is not externally authenticated: a TOFU genesis proves key
   244	 possession, never who holds the key — see the bootstrap below.) This is the arrival
   245	 restatement of the historized-keys
   246	 ruling (internal-table-meta-schema, 2026-07-01): time-correct
   247	 verification, rotation as a new row.
   248	
   249	### The bootstrap: self-certifying genesis
   250	
   251	 Line 1 must be signed by a key the log has not introduced yet. The circularity resolves
   252	 by self-certification: the genesis fact is the founding introduction
   253	 — it carries the public key and is signed by that key, trust-on-first-use at
   254	 line 1, the same shape as an SSH CA root. It is honest about what it proves, because of a
   255	 protected invariant loops already holds — signatures attest claims, not external
   256	 truth. The self-signed introduction attests possession: “the holder of this key
   257	 opened this lineage.” It does not attest that the key belongs to any person; names are
   258	 lineage-local labels, and binding a name to a person across lineages is the vouch arc,
   259	 deliberately deferred. Anyone can mint a lineage claiming any name — that is the
   260	 honest scope of local custody, not a defect.
   261	
   262	 Later observers are introduced by facts signed by an already-introduced observer, so every
   263	 introduction is attributable, and the admission check “source not authorized for
   264	 intake” reads its authorization from the log itself. Keyless genesis stays coherent:
   265	 --allow-unsigned opens the lineage with no self-certifying anchor, the
   266	 classification fact records signature era UNSIGNED, and an introduction
   267	 can open the signed era later — the unsigned→signed transition era-monotonicity
   268	 already permits.
   269	
   270	## 7Rotation, revocation, taint
   271	
   272	Rotation was already ruled. The compromise case was the one true gap, closed this session.
   273	
   274	 The cooperative cases were settled by the 2026-08-14 custody arc and the meta-schema ruling
   275	 before it: rotation within an observer’s key set is invisible to everyone else’s
   276	 grants; removal is a forward-only epoch rekey (“stops seeing new, never unsees
   277	 old”); delegate revocation is certificate expiry, never a registry event; and registry
   278	 mutations must be signed facts, verified before honoring. What no prior pass unpacked:
   279	 what a revocation means for records signed between a key’s compromise and the
   280	 revocation’s position.
   281	
   282	Ruled — arm one, the default
   283	
   284	 Taint stays in place. The records were legitimately admitted — at
   285	 admission time the key was valid, so the receipt was and remains true. Compromise degrades
   286	 the attribution (signature no longer implies author for the span), never the
   287	 receipt; excising records would delete true history to fix a false inference. Excision is
   288	 also the operation arrival exists to forbid — a rebuild-with-holes renumbers every
   289	 later witness position, breaks every seal over the span, and establishes that custody history
   290	 is editable, capping the log’s evidentiary value permanently. The revocation fact gives
   291	 every projection a position-bounded predicate — “signed by K in
   292	 (introduction, revocation)” — to discount, exclude, or flag per purpose,
   293	 reversibly if the compromise window is later re-assessed. Tainted-span disposition
   294	 is interpretation policy: admission records observation, not semantic eligibility, applied to
   295	 keys. The taint is also the forensic record of the incident itself.
   296	
   297	Ruled — arm two, content destruction
   298	
   299	 When tainted content must genuinely cease to exist — a live secret or
   300	 poisonous payload written into the log — the operation is the single re-custody
   301	 ceremony, never an in-place rebuild: a new lineage, genesis receipted with source
   302	 hashes, classification facts recording exactly what was excised and why. Old positions die
   303	 honestly because their lineage is retired, not silently renumbered. Second instance of the
   304	 ceremony beside the 0.x→1.0 sidecar; nothing is built for revocation at all.
   305	
   306	## 8Aggregation membership
   307	
   308	Vertex registration dissolves into declaration state in the aggregator’s own lineage.
   309	
   310	 Membership is not an admission — nothing arrives from a source. “This vertex’s
   311	 answers draw on these lineages” is the custodian’s interpretive commitment, which
   312	 makes it declaration state, changed through the declaration ceremony in the
   313	 aggregator’s own log. The vocabulary already exists: the meta-schema’s CONSTITUTION
   314	 events name member-defined / member-removed as historized
   315	 combine constituents. Today’s config globs and combine blocks appear in no
   316	 receipt anywhere — “what was the root vertex aggregating in June” is
   317	 unanswerable. Under this ruling it is a fold over CONSTITUTION events.
   318	
   319	 | 
   320	 | Where | What | Why there
   321	
   322	 | In the aggregator’s log
   323	 | Membership declarations naming members by lineage id
   324	 | Stable, minted at member genesis, machine-independent; aggregated-read provenance is
   325	 incomplete without it — that is what earns log residence
   326	
   327	 | Outside, driver config
   328	 | The locator map: lineage id → current path / remote / schedule
   329	 | Paths are transport; a log naming filesystem paths lies the moment anything moves
   330	
   331	 | The discover glob
   332	 | Degrades from authority to a discovery driver
   333	 | Scans, reports “lineage X found at path P,” drafts a membership
   334	 declaration edit for the custodian to absorb — Law 3 shape, through the
   335	 declaration ceremony
   336	
   337	 One boundary deliberately unmoved: ad-hoc combine stays registration-free.
   338	 Pointing a read at arbitrary stores remains a pure read-path operation. Membership declarations
   339	 are for standing aggregation, where the composition is part of what answers mean; exploratory
   340	 reads carry no membership provenance, honestly.
   341	
   342	## 9Ceremonies and the CAS
   343	
   344	cas(head) is specified: conditional append on a kind-scoped witness position.
   345	
   344	cas(head) is specified: conditional append on a kind-scoped witness position.
   345	
   346	 The surviving verb set is append(record)→stamp, records_from(pos),
   347	 cas(head) — and the last needed sharpening. A declaration edit guards
   348	 against “a declaration change slipped in since I folded out the file,” which is a
   349	 CAS against the declaration head. CAS against the arrival head would be spuriously
   350	 strict: any ordinary admission between fold-out and re-absorb would fail an edit with no actual
   351	 conflict.
   352	
   353	Two scopes, frozen shrink-only
   354	
   355	 The condition is “append this batch iff no record of kind-class K admitted since
   356	 position N,” with exactly two sanctioned scopes:
   357	 K = everything (the arrival head — transport, backup
   358	 boundaries) and K = declarations (the ceremony guard, what
   359	 _declaration_head_in_txn already computes). No predicate generalization —
   360	 “append-if no record matching P” is a query language growing inside the
   361	 custody boundary, and admission stays too dumb to evaluate semantics. A third scope requires
   362	 demonstrating a ceremony that is structurally neither ordinary admission nor a declaration
   363	 edit — and every ceremony named in this arc (introductions, grants, rotations,
   364	 revocations, membership, epoch mutations) is declaration-shaped, because the observers block
   365	 and membership were both ruled into the declaration document set.
   366	
   367	 Rider: _decl.* promotes from convention to contract-level
   368	 namespace — it binds forever under the one-break budget, said out loud. It is
   369	 structural, not semantic: the custodian distinguishes declaration records the way it
   370	 distinguishes fact lines from tick lines, knowing nothing of what any declaration means. The
   371	 namespace already exists de facto (_decl.genesis, the frozen
   372	 *-defined/*-retired edit vocabulary, _meta.own_lineage,
   373	 _topology); under arrival, _meta.own_lineage moves from
   374	 store_meta into the genesis fact and dies with the derived index.
   375	
   376	 Surface rider: the SDK types the two coordinates as distinct types —
   377	 ArrivalHead and DeclarationHead — never a generic position plus
   378	 a flag, so the frozen scope set lives in the type system rather than in review vigilance. The
   379	 verb renames to guarded_append: cas(head) under-describes semantics
   380	 that are “append iff no record of class K arrived since N,” and an API
   381	 shaped like a generic CAS quietly makes arbitrary predicates look like an obvious extension.
   382	
   383	## 10Integrity posture
   384	
   385	What commits to what — and the honest sentence about what doesn’t.
   386	
   387	 | 
   388	 | Mechanism | Commits to
   389	
   390	 | window_hash | Fact content and fact order between two id-resolved cursor bounds, in line order — facts only
   391	
   392	 | prev_hash | Total order over ticks, chained tick to tick
   393	
   394	 | fact_cursor | Which fact each tick follows — ties among cursor-sharing ticks break by chain order
   395	
   396	 | signature | Authorship of the record’s claim, per observer, position-verified (§6)
   397	
   398	 Windows stay facts-only by force, not preference: signed history migrates exactly iff its
   399	 committed bytes are unchanged, and historical windows commit to fact rows between cursor
   400	 bounds. Including tick lines would change every window’s input and demand re-signing,
   401	 which the exactness ruling forbids. Nothing is lost, and the reconstruction rule is stated
   402	 rather than gestured at: a tick’s cursor assigns it to the gap immediately after
   403	 fact F; ticks sharing a gap order by chain; chain continuity makes cursor assignment
   404	 monotone — given ordered facts, ordered ticks, and per-tick gap assignment,
   405	 exactly one total interleave exists. That is a claim that can be falsified automatically, so it
   406	 must be: interleave uniqueness is a conformance-vector obligation, a candidate
   407	 family for the loops-go oracle queue. (Correcting an overstatement in the substrate memo: no
   408	 rowid was ever inside signed or hashed bytes — ticks commit to fact-id cursors, and
   409	 rowid was only ever the ordering authority. That is why signed history migrates
   410	 without re-signing at all.)
   411	
   412	 The honest residue, accepted deliberately: between seals and on the live edge, the log’s
   413	 only integrity is possession — it is the custodian’s file on the custodian’s
   414	 disk. Seal density is the policy lever: a custodian wanting finer-grained
   415	 commitment seals more often. Per-record chaining was considered and rejected — it defends
   416	 against an adversary with write access to the custody log, who could re-chain regardless, and
   417	 its cost lands on the honest path by making every record’s bytes position-dependent.
   418	
   419	## 11Migration touchpoints
   420	
   421	What this document’s rulings hand the sidecar spec.
   422	
   423	Class (a) is a re-read. The JSONL-canonical flagship store is already in
   424	 the arrival format’s line grammar; migration re-encodes nothing — it prepends
   425	 the genesis ceremony and carries lines verbatim.
   426	
   427	Legacy physical position ≠ 1.0 witness ordinal — even for
   428	 order-verified stores. The genesis ceremony shifts every carried
   429	 record by its own length, and no old integer is claimed: what is preserved is relative
   430	 order, record bytes, and tick relationships — never receipt-number identity. Stated
