01KWA3YH5BHYAS2VN64V2Z612R	architecture/cross-store-ref-resolution			VERIFIED model (2026-06-29, traced + reproduced) — corrects my earlier overstatement that "cross-store refs do not resolve." They do; the navigable address ALWAYS survives. Three layers: (1) EMIT-TIME PIN — _resolve_one walks the root-cwd topology (combine/discover chain) and pins the target current ULID into a {field}_ref sibling; resolves ONLY if the target store is in the emit cwd topology. Out-of-topology emit prints WARN "ref did not resolve — dropped", but that drops only the PIN, not the ref. (2) NAVIGABLE ADDRESS — the kind:key value is preserved verbatim in item.refs regardless of pin success (confirmed: painted seed fact retains [decision:design/completion-dissolves-into-painted, ...] in refs after a "dropped" WARN). (3) READ-SIDE GRAPH — _compute_inbound_edges is ADDRESS-keyed (kind/key, colon/slash normalized) and store-local to the READ. CONSEQUENCE: a cross-repo ref resolves in the GRAPH when read through a topology that combines both ends — reproduced: loops target inbound=1 via the cli-completion aggregation (combines loops+painted), inbound=0 read loops-alone (harmless dangling). So cross-store ref resolution is a TOPOLOGY-MEMBERSHIP property (a combine-block config choice), not a hard limit — this is the basis for the on-demand arc-aggregation pattern. CAVEAT: the emit-time pin is a ULID snapshot, fragile across independent store rebirth; the address is durable and the graph runs on it, so a dangling pin is cosmetic-provenance, not a lost edge.
01KWF7DDNFVBSKYVMWNFB2JBER	typed-edges-declared-vs-implicit	open		Finding out of agent-attestation RFC work: resolution is already field-agnostic (any address-valued field pins {field}_ref) but the fold's _refs lift is ref-only, so typed edges (superseded_by=, a proposed stakeholder=) resolve+pin yet never enter salience/--refs. Real question isn't 'add typed edges' -- it's (a) ref is the degenerate predicate=ref case of a typed edge and the fold is the last place it's special-cased, so generalizing FINISHES a half-built dissolution, and (b) reconciles the live emit/fold split above. Design fork: implicit (value-shape heuristic decides edge membership) vs declared (edge-field in KDL). Leaning DECLARED: closes the footgun structurally, gives predicate labels + bare-key ergonomics, dissolves 3 fragile resolve guards into 1 declaration; ref grandfathered. Architecture: materialize at the resolve seam (already has topology+edge set) via a generic reserved _edges field atoms collects -- NOT by threading schema into the pure atoms fold factory. Costs: raw-address-not-ULID form, bare-key --refs walkability, single-vs-multi cardinality, golden churn on arrow-N/density/JSON.
01KWFEV3A1PXG70QS062FB8DXP	architecture/typed-edges-late-bound-promotion			Kyle's requirement reframes the typed-edge design: capture must be declaration-free and in-the-moment (emit stakeholder=X naturally during a session); declaration comes LATER as a vertex edit that 'shows' the edge. Consequences: (1) PIN and EDGE separate, with different binding times. The {field}_ref ULID pin stays emit-time / implicit / best-effort -- a provenance snapshot ('at emit this address pointed at ULID X'). Graph EDGE-membership (salience, --refs, predicate label) is declaration-gated. (2) Promotion is RETROACTIVE, therefore READ-TIME: facts are immutable and the raw address is already persisted, so declaring 'edge <field> targets=<kind>' later lights up ALL historical facts carrying that field -- zero re-emit. (3) This MOVES the build seam. Prior turn I said materialize at the resolve/emit path; late-binding kills that (it would freeze the ontology at capture). Seam moves to FOLD CONSTRUCTION: thread the edge declaration lang->atoms into _make_upsert/_make_collect so collection reflects the CURRENT vertex over all history. (4) Footgun dissolves without gating capture -- accidental captures (topic_ref) stay inert unless someone declares them an edge. Net: schema-on-read / late-bound edge ontology; same capture(reflex) / reconcile(review) rhythm as the rest of loops. Open: retroactive promotion silently re-ranks historical salience -> likely wants a --dry-run preview on declaration.
01KWFSX69C5BCN0Q9HFDG53T8J	vertex-state-in-store	open		Taxonomy for the internal table (Kyle+Fable 2026-07-01), from the framing 'the .vertex is the definition of this topology of semantic attention.' Membership test: could two readers at different TIMES disagree about the same facts because this changed? If yes, historize. Three strata all pass: (1) INTERPRETATION — kinds, fold ops, edge decls, boundary decls, search decls: what facts MEAN. (2) CONSTITUTION — combine/discover membership, peers, grants, observer registry/keys: whose facts and which stores CONSTITUTE the view. (3) ATTESTATION — ticks, seals: already historized, the precedent. Excluded by the same test: lenses and preview fields (change pixels, not fold/salience/traversal — a rewound fold under today's lens is still honest); hooks (harness, not store semantics). Two structural findings: (a) aggregation vertices own NO store today, so their constitution history (combine membership over time) has no home — implies every vertex, even aggregations, needs at least an internal table; rewinding an aggregate without membership history is dishonest the same way rewinding under today's ontology is. (b) The regress terminates one level up: internal-table rows are kind-ed by a FIXED code-defined meta-schema (vertex-state, membership-change, grant-change, tick), not by the vertex itself — the store describes its own attention topology over time, but the language it describes it IN is frozen, so no infinite regress.
