    30	    canonical: Path, *, derived_log: bool = False
    31	) -> Rederivation: ...
    32	
    33	def rows_of_record(record: dict) -> list[tuple[str, tuple]]: ...
    34	```
    35	
    36	`rederive_projections` opens **its own sqlite connection** to
    37	`index_path_for(canonical)` and its own `ArrivalLog`. It **never constructs an
    38	`ArrivalStore`**, and `ArrivalStore` gains **no** `rederive` method.
    39	
    40	**Why a module function and not a method (NON-NEGOTIABLE).** The states this
    41	verb resolves are exactly the states `ArrivalStore.__init__` refuses
    42	(`arrival_store.py:157` runs `catch_up()` inside the constructor, and
    43	`catch_up` raises `ArrivalCanonicalUnsupported` at `:236-260`). A method on the
    44	class would be unreachable on the very stores that need it — you cannot
    45	construct the object. Making the constructor tolerate the refusal (a
    46	`park`/`defer` flag) would mean a store that opens in a broken state, which is
    47	precisely what the consume-or-refuse posture forbids. The precedent is
    48	`canonical_audit`'s "pure reader, by contract" (`canonical_audit.py:13-20`):
    49	the module that must not repair never constructs the store that does. Here it
    50	is the inverse and the same rule — the module that repairs never constructs the
    51	store that refuses.
    52	
    53	Second consequence, and the reason it is worth the module: **no write path can
    54	call re-derivation as a side effect.** It is not on the object the write path
    55	holds.
    56	
    57	**Which of the three catch-up refusals resolve into it.** None of them stop
    58	being refusals. All three change from dead ends into refusals that **name the
    59	verb**:
    60	
    61	| `arrival_store.py` | State | After cut B |
    62	|---|---|---|
    63	| `:236-242` | index carries state, **no log** | **Stays a dead end.** No verb offered. Re-derivation cannot manufacture a log; offering it here would name an operation that destroys the only surviving artifact. |
    64	| `:245-251` | rows present, **no mark** | Refuses, and names `rederive_projections`. |
    65	| `:254-260` | mark the log rejects | Refuses, and names `rederive_projections`. |
    66	
    67	Grounding for keeping them refusals: the ratified posture is "re-derivation is
    68	an explicit verb, never an open-time side effect for a store that carries
    69	state". The distinction that survives untouched is `arrival_store.py:47-49` —
    70	**an absent projection builds automatically** (`ensure_arrival_index`,
    71	`:497-521`, and the `mark is None and not _has_rows()` arm), because building
    72	an absent projection destroys nothing. An *existing* projection with rows is
    73	evidence, and discarding evidence is an operator's decision.
    74	
    75	**What survives a re-derivation.**
    76	
    77	- `facts`, `ticks`: cleared and rebuilt from the log, ordinal 0 forward.
    78	- `facts_fts`, `fts_state`: **dropped**, exactly as `jsonl_store._rebuild`
    79	  does and for the identical reason (`jsonl_store.py:945-953`: `DELETE FROM
    80	  facts` resets the rowid counter, so a surviving FTS index resolves stale
    81	  text to new facts). Search then reports `missing`, the honest "run reindex"
    82	  state.
    83	- `store_meta.own_lineage`: **restamped from the log** — see Q5.
    84	- The resume mark (`arrival_lineage`/`arrival_offset`/`arrival_ordinal`):
    85	  restamped to the last consumed record.
    86	- Every other `store_meta` row: preserved. Same rule as
    87	  `jsonl_store.py:942-944` — `store_meta` holds identity, not fact.
    88	
    89	**Live write lock.** `rederive_projections` takes **the sqlite write lock only**
    90	(`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` for the whole clear-and-replay), and **does not take the
    91	arrival append lock**. Rationale, both halves:
    92	
    93	- Taking sqlite's write lock serializes it against every `ArrivalStore._write`
    94	  (`arrival_store.py:366` stages its INSERT under the same lock), so no
    95	  appender can interleave an index write into a half-replayed index.
    96	- Not taking the append lock means an appender may land record N+1 mid-walk.
    97	  That is harmless: the walk stops at the last complete record
    98	  (`arrival.py:_verify_from` returns on an unterminated line, and a newly
    99	  fsync'd complete record simply is or is not seen), and the stamped mark
   100	  names what was actually consumed. The result is the ordinary "index behind
   101	  the log" state, which the next `_reconcile` (`arrival_store.py:307-324`)
   102	  tails forward. Holding the append lock for an O(n) replay would block every
   103	  writer in the system for the duration, to buy a property the mark already
   104	  provides.
